OAI_PAPER({"slug": "Exact-Derandomization-of-Logarithmic-Space-L-equals-RL-equals-BPL-September-23-2026", "title": "Exact derandomization of logarithmic space: L = RL = BPL", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/Exact-Derandomization-of-Logarithmic-Space-L-equals-RL-equals-BPL-September-23-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "103", "family_title": "Exact derandomization of logarithmic space: \\(\\mathsf L=\\mathsf{RL}=\\mathsf{BPL}\\)", "abstract_html": "We prove \\(\\mathsf L=\\mathsf{RL}=\\mathsf{BPL}\\), resolving the derandomization problem for polynomial-time randomized logarithmic space.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Theorem 1.1, “Exact logarithmic-space derandomization.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( \\mathrm L=\\mathrm{RL}=\\mathrm{BPL}. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Machines have finite control, an endmarked read-only input, finitely many work tapes, and fresh independent fair random bits. Physical input heads stay between the endmarkers.</p><p class=\"li\">Space counts traversed writable cells in bits, including all simultaneously live counters, numerical registers, and suspended calls. Visiting a blank work cell counts toward space.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\mathrm L\\) consists of languages decided by total deterministic machines using \\(O(\\log(n+2))\\) work space.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\mathrm{RL}\\) permits polynomial worst-case running time, \\(O(\\log(n+2))\\) work space, acceptance probability \\(0\\) on no inputs, and acceptance probability at least \\(1/2\\) on yes inputs.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\mathrm{BPL}\\) has the same resource bounds and acceptance probabilities at most \\(1/3\\) on no inputs and at least \\(2/3\\) on yes inputs. Randomized time bounds hold on every random tape.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\mathrm L=\\mathrm{RL}=\\mathrm{BPL}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(\\mathcal M\\) decide a \\(\\mathrm{BPL}\\) language. Proposition 10 with \\(d=3\\) gives error at most \\((n+2)^{-3}\\le1/8\\), including \\(n=0\\). Its answer is at most\n\\[\n1/3+1/8=11/24\n\\]\non no inputs and at least\n\\[\n2/3-1/8=13/24\n\\]\non yes inputs. Comparison with \\(1/2\\) gives a total logarithmic-space decider, proving \\(\\mathrm{BPL}\\subseteq\\mathrm L\\).</p><p>A deterministic machine can ignore its coins, so \\(\\mathrm L\\subseteq\\mathrm{RL}\\). Two independent repetitions of an \\(\\mathrm{RL}\\) machine, accepting if either accepts, preserve zero acceptance on no inputs and give at least \\(3/4\\) on yes inputs with logarithmic space and polynomial time. Thus \\(\\mathrm{RL}\\subseteq\\mathrm{BPL}\\), completing the equality.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 2", "orig": "Theorem 1.2, “Acceptance-probability approximation.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is a uniform deterministic algorithm outputting an integer \\(0\\le a\\le2^{q+2}\\) such that \\( \\left|a2^{-(q+2)}-p_{\\mathcal M}(x)\\right|\\le2^{-q}. \\) Its work space is \\(O(\\log(n+2)+q)\\), and its running time is …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix a randomized machine \\(\\mathcal M\\) with polynomial worst-case running time and \\(O(\\log(n+2))\\) work space, under the conventions of Theorem 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(p_{\\mathcal M}(x)\\) be its acceptance probability on an input \\(x\\) of length \\(n\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The precision request is unary: the algorithm receives \\((x,1^q)\\), where \\(q\\ge1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A transducer has a one-way write-only output tape; output contents are not work memory.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a uniform deterministic algorithm outputting an integer \\(0\\le a\\le2^{q+2}\\) such that\n\\[\n\\left|a2^{-(q+2)}-p_{\\mathcal M}(x)\\right|\\le2^{-q}.\n\\]\nIts work space is \\(O(\\log(n+2)+q)\\), and its running time is\n\\[\n(n+2)^{O(1)}2^{O(q)}.\n\\]\nThe constants depend only on \\(\\mathcal M\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix one padded machine \\(\\mathcal M_{\\mathrm{pad}}\\): on a string beginning \\(1^n0x\\), \\(|x|=n\\), simulate \\(\\mathcal M(x)\\) and ignore the suffix; reject absent or malformed prefix data. This is one polynomial-time randomized logarithmic-space machine. Fix Proposition 10's exponent-one approximator for it.</p><p>On \\((x,1^q)\\), set\n\\[\nm=2^{q+\\lceil\\log(2n+4)\\rceil+4},\n\\qquad y=1^n0x0^{m-(2n+1)}.\n\\]\nSimulate the fixed approximator on this virtual length-\\(m\\) input. Answer each virtual read by boundary comparisons and, when required, a scan of real \\(x\\). Store no copy of \\(y\\).</p><p>The returned dyadic \\(z\\in[0,1]\\) satisfies\n\\[\n|z-p_{\\mathcal M}(x)|\\le(m+2)^{-1}&lt;2^{-q-4}.\n\\]\nCapture its \\(O(\\log m)\\)-bit numerator and exponent, and compute exactly\n\\[\na=\\left\\lfloor2^{q+2}z+\\tfrac12\\right\\rfloor.\n\\]\nThen \\(0\\le a\\le2^{q+2}\\), and\n\\[\n|a2^{-(q+2)}-p_{\\mathcal M}(x)|\n\\le2^{-q-3}+2^{-q-4}&lt;2^{-q}.\n\\]</p><p>Simulator state, virtual coordinates, actual input counters, captured output, and rounding operands use\n\\[\nO(\\log m)=O(\\log(n+2)+q)\n\\]\nspace. Time is polynomial in \\(m\\), hence \\((n+2)^{O(1)}2^{O(q)}\\). The padded source and approximator were fixed before reading \\(q\\), so constants do not depend on requested precision. Numerical precision alone was not used to reduce statistical error; padding maintains the proved \\(M_{\\mathrm{top}}=O(B)\\) regime.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 3", "orig": "Corollary 12.3", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( \\mathrm{PromiseBPL}=\\mathrm{PromiseL}. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">A promise problem is a pair \\((Y,N)\\) of disjoint sets.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\mathrm{PromiseBPL}\\), also denoted \\(\\mathrm{prBPL}\\), requires one randomized logarithmic-space machine with polynomial worst-case running time that halts on every input, accepts inputs in \\(Y\\) with probability at least \\(2/3\\), and accepts inputs in \\(N\\) with probability at most \\(1/3\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\mathrm{PromiseL}\\) consists of pairs separated by a total deterministic logarithmic-space decider. Correctness is not required outside \\(Y\\cup N\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\mathrm{PromiseBPL}=\\mathrm{PromiseL}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply Proposition 10 with \\(d=3\\) to the total randomized promise machine, and compare its dyadic approximation with \\(1/2\\), as in Theorem 1's proof. The \\(1/8\\) error separates every input in \\(Y\\) from every input in \\(N\\). The approximator is total also outside the promise, so the resulting separator is a total logarithmic-space decider. The reverse inclusion follows by ignoring random bits.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 4", "orig": "Corollary 12.4, “Certified accepting computations.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is a uniform deterministic logarithmic-space, polynomial-time transducer that outputs an accepting computation of \\(\\mathcal M\\) whenever \\( p_{\\mathcal M}(x)\\ge(n+2)^{-c}. \\) On every other input it outputs …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix a randomized logarithmic-space machine \\(\\mathcal M\\) that halts within a fixed polynomial bound \\(T(n)\\ge1\\) on every input and every coin sequence.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix an integer \\(c\\ge0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For the witness conclusion, \\(\\mathcal M\\)'s output tape is write-only, so previously emitted symbols do not affect subsequent transitions.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a uniform deterministic logarithmic-space, polynomial-time transducer that outputs an accepting computation of \\(\\mathcal M\\) whenever\n\\[\np_{\\mathcal M}(x)\\ge(n+2)^{-c}.\n\\]\nOn every other input it outputs either an accepting computation or a failure symbol.</p><p>If every accepting run emits a valid witness for a specified relation, the transducer can instead output such a witness with the same guarantee.</p>", "proof": "<p>Give halted configurations artificial self-transitions through time \\(T(n)\\). A configuration and elapsed time have \\(O(\\log(n+2))\\)-bit encodings. Fix a randomized logarithmic-space continuation machine on encodings \\((x,v,t)\\): validate the work box and time, then simulate the remaining \\(T(n)-t\\) steps with fresh coins, rejecting malformed data or violations. On reachable states its acceptance probability is the continuation value \\(p_t(v)\\).</p><p>Let \\(\\gamma=(n+2)^{-c}\\). Choose a fixed exponent \\(d\\) so Proposition 10 estimates each queried continuation value within\n\\[\n\\delta\\le\\gamma/(8T(n)).\n\\]\nThe continuation input contains \\(x\\), and its additional fields are logarithmic. Supply those fields virtually from saved registers.</p><p>Starting from the initial configuration, estimate every positive-probability successor and choose the maximum estimate, with fixed port ties. Because the current value is the weighted mean of successor values, the chosen \\(v'\\) satisfies\n\\[\np_{t+1}(v')\\ge\\max_wp_{t+1}(w)-2\\delta\n\\ge p_t(v)-2\\delta.\n\\]\nIf \\(p_{\\mathcal M}(x)\\ge\\gamma\\), the terminal continuation value is at least\n\\[\n\\gamma-2T(n)\\delta\\ge3\\gamma/4&gt;0.\n\\]\nA terminal value is zero or one, so this run accepts.</p><p>First perform the construction without output. If it rejects, emit failure. Otherwise repeat the identical deterministic choices and stream configurations through the original halt, or stream the emitted witness symbols. Omit artificial post-halt transitions. Thus even outside the promise no rejecting computation is output.</p><p>Current, candidate, and best configurations, time, a constant number of dyadic scores, virtual coordinates, and approximation workspace occupy logarithmic space simultaneously. Constant successor degree and polynomially many continuation estimates give polynomial time. Every query has a deterministic approximation guarantee; no common accuracy event for adaptive queries is required.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 5", "orig": "Theorem 13.1, “Effective simulation with explicit bounds.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "One terminating algorithm emits a deterministic machine \\(D_{\\mathcal M}\\) and positive integers \\(K,H,c\\) such that, when the source promise holds, \\(D_{\\mathcal M}\\) decides the same language and its execution on …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">The compiler receives a finite randomized machine description \\(\\mathcal M\\) and positive integers \\(a,b\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The supplied bounds are hypotheses on the source, not properties that the compiler must decide.</p><p class=\"li\">The source promise is that, on every input of length \\(n\\) and every random tape, \\(\\mathcal M\\) halts within \\((n+2)^a\\) steps, uses at most \\(b\\lceil\\log(n+2)\\rceil\\) work bits, and has acceptance probability at most \\(1/3\\) on no inputs and at least \\(2/3\\) on yes inputs.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>One terminating algorithm emits a deterministic machine \\(D_{\\mathcal M}\\) and positive integers \\(K,H,c\\) such that, when the source promise holds, \\(D_{\\mathcal M}\\) decides the same language and its execution on every input uses at most\n\\[\nK\\log(n+2)\n\\]\nwork bits and\n\\[\nH(n+2)^c\n\\]\nelementary bit operations.</p><p>The compiler's running time need only be finite; its output exponent may depend on the source.</p>", "proof": "<p>Use Proposition 11's fixed grammar, capped interpreter \\(U\\), and total deterministic threshold separator \\(A\\). Given \\((\\mathcal M,a,b)\\), convert the source effectively without increasing its interpreted transition count or counted work usage, construct its code \\(z\\), compute\n\\[\nP=1000(d_{\\mathrm{code}}+2)^4(a+b+2),\n\\]\nand emit the virtual-input specialization \\(D_{\\mathcal M}\\).</p><p>When the supplied source promises hold, Lemma 11.2 gives exact equality of source and interpreter acceptance probabilities on the padded instance. The fixed separator therefore makes the required decision. Its totality and the finite callback loops give totality of the generated machine, independently of source promises.</p><p>Return the syntax-computed integers \\(K,H,c\\) of Lemma 11.3. They bound the same execution's counted bit space and elementary bit time, including the library while callbacks are active. All finite construction steps terminate; no semantic inference about the source is required. The conclusions include \\(n=0\\), where \\(B_n=1\\).</p><p>The internal probability-estimation work—environment enumeration, recursive interfaces, recomputation, digits, and precision management—belongs to the one complete library already bounded in Proposition 10. The compiler adds only the explicitly analyzed virtual input operations, not an oracle or external stack.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The machine has a uniformly logarithmic-space-accessible strictly forward substochastic configuration matrix \\(S\\), terminal reward \\(e\\), and start \\(x_0\\), with …", "statement": "<p>Configuration model and retained-set channels.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix a polynomial-time randomized logarithmic-space machine and an input of length \\(n\\).</p><p class=\"li\">All logarithms have base two.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(B\\) be a power of two, at least a sufficiently large fixed constant and comparable with \\(\\log(n+2)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix integers \\(H\\ge4\\) and \\(2\\le h_0&lt;H\\), and define\n\\[\nb_k=2^{-Hk}\\qquad(k\\ge0).\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Identifiers are injective numerical encodings. A base identifier is a fixed-width radix-\\(D_0\\) word prefixed by \\(1\\); a stage-\\(l\\) identifier appends \\(l\\) copy symbols. A common fixed suffix acts affinely on numerical identifiers.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(P\\) be prime, larger than all identifiers and sufficiently large for every requested rate. Write \\(r=h_0-1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Each rank channel is an independent uniformly chosen\n\\[\nA=(a_0,F)\\in\\mathbb F_P^{H\\times h_0}\n\\]\nconditioned on the \\(r\\) columns of \\(F\\) being linearly independent.</p><p class=\"li\">Set\n\\[\nv(y)=(1,y,\\ldots,y^{h_0-1})^{\\mathsf T},\n\\quad a(y)=Av(y),\n\\quad L_k=\\lceil P2^{-k}\\rceil,\n\\quad \\pi_{b_k}=(L_k/P)^H.\n\\]\nAn identifier is retained at rate \\(b_k\\) when every coordinate of \\(a(y)\\), in its standard integer representative, lies in \\(\\{0,\\ldots,L_k-1\\}\\). Assume \\(P2^{-k}\\ge1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The machine has a uniformly logarithmic-space-accessible strictly forward substochastic configuration matrix \\(S\\), terminal reward \\(e\\), and start \\(x_0\\), with\n\\[\np_0=(I-S)^{-1}e\\in[0,1]^{V_0},\n\\qquad p_0(x_0)=p_{\\mathcal M}(x).\n\\]\nIts incoming and outgoing port degrees are bounded constants. The rank channels have uniform marginal, conditional-pair, lower-tail, and affine-invariance estimates as specified in Lemma 1.2.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 1.1 supplies the finite inverse model and local paired configuration ports. Lemma 1.2 supplies the retained-set estimates independently of the ambient identifier range.</p><p>Stage-\\(l\\) identifiers lie in disjoint intervals\n\\[\n[D_0^{w+l},2D_0^{w+l}),\n\\]\nwhere \\(w\\) is the base width. Appending a suffix is affine. For \\(L=O(B)\\), all stage domains have size at most a common bound \\(N=2^{C_NB}\\), after fixing \\(C_N\\). Field elements and identifiers use \\(O(B)\\) bits when \\(P=2^{O(B)}\\). The later choice of field exponent does not alter the channel constants.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "Lemma 2.1, “Configuration reduction.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "In the machine setting of Proposition 1, one can specify uniformly in logarithmic space a polynomial-size vertex set \\(V_0\\), a strictly forward substochastic matrix \\(S\\), a vector \\(e\\in\\{0,1\\}^{V_0}\\), and a start …", "statement": "<p>In the machine setting of Proposition 1, one can specify uniformly in logarithmic space a polynomial-size vertex set \\(V_0\\), a strictly forward substochastic matrix \\(S\\), a vector \\(e\\in\\{0,1\\}^{V_0}\\), and a start \\(x_0\\) such that:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. Incoming and outgoing port degrees are bounded constants depending only on the machine; validity, weights, and port pairing are logarithmic-space computable.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. \\(p_0=(I-S)^{-1}e\\) is well defined and belongs to \\([0,1]^{V_0}\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">3. \\(p_0(x_0)\\) is the acceptance probability.</p><p>Here “strictly forward” means that every nonzero entry advances an attached integer time, and “substochastic” means nonnegative with row sums at most one.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(T(n)\\) be a fixed polynomial worst-case time bound. A vertex records time in \\(\\{0,\\ldots,T(n)\\}\\), finite control, all input and work-head positions, and the contents of a sufficiently large \\(O(\\log(n+2))\\)-bit work box. Elementary transitions have fixed dyadic coin probabilities and advance time by one. Invalid configurations and moves leaving the box have no transition; this never affects a computation from the specified start.</p><p>There are constantly many successor choices. A predecessor is found by guessing the previous control, overwritten local symbols, head movements, and coin choice, and checking the forward step. Combine duplicate endpoint pairs by summing their probabilities. Ordering distinct successor and predecessor encodings gives paired ports. These constant-size lists require only logarithmic space and no recursive graph queries.</p><p>Input-head coordinates range over a fixed polynomial interval containing all positions reachable in \\(T(n)\\) local steps. To read a simulated coordinate, compare it with the input boundaries, supply the appropriate marker or off-data blank, or scan the physical input to the designated data position. The physical head remains between the real endmarkers.</p><p>A halted configuration retains its complete tape and head data while time advances; halted configurations are not merged. Let \\(e\\) indicate accepting configurations at time \\(T(n)\\). Final-time rows have no outgoing transitions. Nilpotence gives\n\\[\n(I-S)^{-1}e=\\sum_{j=0}^{T(n)}S^je.\n\\]\nStarting from any valid configuration, at most one reward is collected, at final time. Thus every coordinate is a probability in \\([0,1]\\), and the start coordinate is the machine's acceptance probability.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.2", "orig": "Lemma 3.1, “Rank-channel estimates.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Under the rank-channel assumptions of Proposition 1, there are positive constants \\(c_\\pi,C_\\pi,C_{\\mathrm{pair}},C_{\\mathrm{var}}\\), depending only on \\(H,h_0\\), such that:", "statement": "<p>Under the rank-channel assumptions of Proposition 1, there are positive constants \\(c_\\pi,C_\\pi,C_{\\mathrm{pair}},C_{\\mathrm{var}}\\), depending only on \\(H,h_0\\), such that:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. A specified identifier is retained at rate \\(a\\) with probability \\(\\pi_a\\), where\n\\[\nc_\\pi a\\le\\pi_a\\le C_\\pi a.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li num\">2. For distinct identifiers \\(x,y\\) and admissible rates \\(u,a\\),\n\\[\n\\Pr[X_a(y)=1\\mid X_u(x)=1]\\le C_{\\mathrm{pair}}a.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li num\">3. If \\(A_0\\) is a deterministic set of \\(q&gt;0\\) distinct identifiers and \\(T_a=\\sum_{y\\in A_0}X_a(y)\\), then\n\\[\n\\Pr[T_a&lt;q\\pi_a/2]\\le\\frac{C_{\\mathrm{var}}}{q\\pi_a}.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li num\">4. Replacing all arguments by the same injective affine map \\(y\\mapsto\\alpha y+\\beta\\), with \\(\\alpha\\ne0\\) in \\(\\mathbb F_P\\), preserves the joint law of evaluations. In particular, common fixed padding suffixes preserve that law.</p>", "proof": "<p>An unrestricted \\(H\\times r\\) matrix is a frame with probability\n\\[\nq_{\\mathrm{fr}}=\\prod_{j=0}^{r-1}(1-P^{j-H})\\ge c_{\\mathrm{fr}}&gt;0.\n\\]\nFor fixed positive-degree coefficients, the constant column is uniform, so one evaluation is uniform in \\(\\mathbb F_P^H\\) and independent of frame success. This proves the exact marginal. Since\n\\[\nP2^{-k}\\le L_k\\le2P2^{-k},\n\\]\nthe marginal is comparable with \\(b_k\\).</p><p>Without frame conditioning, two evaluations at distinct points are independent uniform vectors: their evaluation functionals have rank two because \\(h_0\\ge2\\). Therefore\n\\[\n\\Pr[X_a(y)=1,X_u(x)=1\\mid F\\text{ is a frame}]\n\\le\\frac{\\pi_a\\pi_u}{q_{\\mathrm{fr}}}.\n\\]\nThe first marginal after conditioning remains \\(\\pi_u\\), proving the pair bound.</p><p>In the unrestricted distribution, pairwise independence gives mean \\(q\\pi_a\\) and variance at most \\(q\\pi_a\\) for the hit count. Chebyshev gives \\(4/(q\\pi_a)\\); frame conditioning increases this by at most \\(1/q_{\\mathrm{fr}}\\).</p><p>Finally, substituting \\(\\alpha y+\\beta\\) acts invertibly and triangularly on positive-degree columns, with diagonal \\(\\alpha,\\ldots,\\alpha^{h_0-1}\\), and invertibly on the full coefficient array. It preserves both the frame condition and its uniform conditional measure. Appending radix-\\(D_0\\) digits has \\(\\alpha\\) a power of \\(D_0\\), which is invertible when \\(P&gt;D_0\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There are fixed hierarchy constants such that one stage replaces \\(C\\) by a strictly forward substochastic matrix \\(C^+\\) on \\(V\\times\\{0,\\ldots,D_0-1\\}\\) satisfying \\( C^+(x_i,y_j)\\le f/q,\\qquad |A(C^+)|\\le2|A(C)|, \\) …", "statement": "<p>Exact correction, copies, and reward transport.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(C\\) be a nonnegative matrix on a finite ordered set \\(V\\) satisfying\n\\[\nC(x,y)=0\\text{ unless }x&lt;y,\\qquad C\\mathbf1\\le\\mathbf1,\\qquad C(x,y)\\le f,\n\\]\nwhere \\(f\\) is on the rate grid of Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(q=2^H\\). Choose rational\n\\[\n0&lt;\\theta_1&lt;\\theta_2&lt;q^{-1}/32.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(Q\\) be nondecreasing and Lipschitz, extended by zero to negative arguments, with\n\\[\n(t-\\theta_2)_+\\le Q(t)\\le(t-\\theta_1)_+,\n\\]\nand with Lipschitz eighth root on bounded intervals.</p><p class=\"li\">Such cutoffs, and the switches below, are chosen piecewise polynomial with rational coefficients. One choice uses the normalized integral of \\(t^7(1-t)^7\\) on \\([0,1]\\).</p><p class=\"li\">All detector expectations below are true finite expectations, not estimates used to define their own targets.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are fixed hierarchy constants such that one stage replaces \\(C\\) by a strictly forward substochastic matrix \\(C^+\\) on \\(V\\times\\{0,\\ldots,D_0-1\\}\\) satisfying\n\\[\nC^+(x_i,y_j)\\le f/q,\\qquad |A(C^+)|\\le2|A(C)|,\n\\]\nwhere \\(A(C)\\) is the union of the nonzero row and column supports.</p><p>Iterating from \\(C_0=S\\) gives nonnegative rewards \\(W_l\\le1\\) and cumulative weighted projections \\(P_l^c\\) such that\n\\[\nW_l=P_l^cp_0-C_lP_l^cp_0.\n\\]\nAt the all-primary copy \\(\\bar x_l\\) of a base vertex \\(x\\),\n\\[\n0\\le p_0(x)-W_l(\\bar x_l)\n\\le |V_0|2^{-(H-1)l}.\n\\]\nThus \\(L=O(B)\\) stages suffice for any fixed inverse-polynomial accuracy, and every stage has \\(O(B)\\)-bit addresses.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas 2.1–2.4 give a fixed finite correction with nonnegative substochastic residual, small entries on fully enabled columns, and domination of its unsigned defining terms. Lemma 2.5 distributes the remaining large entries among copies while at most doubling the active set. Lemma 2.6 transports the reward through those same weighted projections and bounds the residual transition contribution. Increasing the constant in \\(L=O(B)\\) gives any fixed inverse-polynomial error. The fixed point \\(E_*\\) is only a comparison object; the hierarchy uses the finite pilot and actual iterations.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "Lemma 4.1, “Fixed point and factorial truncation.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "In Proposition 2's setting, fix a column multiplier \\(g:V\\to[0,1]\\), and define \\( E_0=0,\\qquad G_i'=C+E_{i-1}C,\\qquad E_i(x,y)=fQ(G_i'(x,y)/f)g(y). \\) There is a unique strictly forward nonnegative solution …", "statement": "<p>In Proposition 2's setting, fix a column multiplier \\(g:V\\to[0,1]\\), and define\n\\[\nE_0=0,\\qquad\nG_i'=C+E_{i-1}C,\\qquad\nE_i(x,y)=fQ(G_i'(x,y)/f)g(y).\n\\]\nThere is a unique strictly forward nonnegative solution\n\\[\nE_*(x,y)=fQ((C+E_*C)(x,y)/f)g(y).\n\\]\nMoreover, \\(0\\le E_i\\le E_*\\), and for every \\(k\\ge0\\),\n\\[\n\\max_{x,y}\\frac{E_*(x,y)-E_k(x,y)}f\n\\le e^{1/\\theta_1}\\frac{(L_Q/\\theta_1)^k}{k!},\n\\]\nwhere \\(L_Q\\) is a Lipschitz constant for \\(Q\\).</p><p>A fixed integer \\(k_*\\), independent of \\(C,f,V,g\\), makes the right side at most \\(q^{-1}/32\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For a fixed row \\(x\\), \\((E_*C)(x,y)\\) involves only entries \\(E_*(x,z)\\) with \\(z&lt;y\\). The fixed-point equation therefore defines the row successively, proving existence and uniqueness. Monotonicity gives \\(E_i\\le E_*\\).</p><p>Set \\(T=C-E_*+E_*C\\). If \\(a=(C+E_*C)(x,y)\\), then\n\\[\n0\\le E_*(x,y)\\le(a-\\theta_1f)_+.\n\\]\nThus \\(T\\ge0\\), and \\(T(x,y)\\ge\\theta_1f\\) wherever \\(E_*(x,y)&gt;0\\). Also\n\\[\nT\\mathbf1=C\\mathbf1-E_*(\\mathbf1-C\\mathbf1)\\le\\mathbf1.\n\\]\nConsequently the support \\(A_x=\\{y:E_*(x,y)&gt;0\\}\\) has size \\(m_x\\le1/(\\theta_1f)\\).</p><p>Unroll \\(E_*\\le C+E_*C\\) along this single row. Every resulting term uses a strictly increasing sequence\n\\[\nx&lt;z_1&lt;\\cdots&lt;z_j&lt;y,\\qquad z_i\\in A_x.\n\\]\nThere are at most \\(\\binom{m_x}{j}\\) such sequences, each with product at most \\(f^{j+1}\\). Hence\n\\[\nE_*(x,y)\\le f(1+f)^{m_x}\\le fe^{1/\\theta_1}.\n\\]</p><p>For \\(\\Delta_i=E_*-E_i\\), the Lipschitz inequality gives\n\\[\n\\Delta_i(x,y)\\le L_Q\\sum_{z&lt;y}\\Delta_{i-1}(x,z)C(z,y).\n\\]\nThe differences are supported in \\(A_x\\). Unrolling \\(k\\) times therefore gives\n\\[\n\\Delta_k(x,y)\n\\le fe^{1/\\theta_1}L_Q^kf^k\\binom{m_x}{k}\n\\le fe^{1/\\theta_1}\\frac{(L_Q/\\theta_1)^k}{k!}.\n\\]\nFor \\(k&gt;m_x\\), the first bound is zero. The factorial eventually dominates the fixed exponential factor, yielding \\(k_*\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.2", "orig": "Lemma 4.2, “Elementary pilot bounds.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "In Proposition 2's setting, fix the \\(k_*\\) of Lemma 2.1.", "statement": "<p>In Proposition 2's setting, fix the \\(k_*\\) of Lemma 2.1. Choose\n\\[\n0&lt;\\delta_0&lt;\\delta_1&lt;\\theta_1/100,\\qquad\n0&lt;\\eta_-&lt;\\eta_+&lt;\\delta_0/3.\n\\]\nLet \\(\\varphi\\) rise from zero through \\(\\delta_0\\) to one from \\(\\delta_1\\); let \\(\\rho\\) fall from one through \\(\\eta_-\\) to zero from \\(\\eta_+\\); and let \\(\\gamma:[0,1]\\to[0,1]\\) be nonincreasing, one through \\(1/2\\), and zero from \\(4/5\\).</p><p>For independent channels \\(X,Y\\), define the pilot by\n\\[\n\\widehat E_0=0,\\qquad G_i=C+\\widehat E_{i-1}C,\n\\]\n\\[\nR_i(x)=\\rho\\!\\left(\\operatorname{rank}_K\n\\{G_i(x,w)/f:Y_f(w)=1\\}\\right),\n\\]\n\\[\nU_i(y)=\n\\mathbb E\\!\\left[\n\\operatorname{rank}_J\n\\{\\varphi(G_i(x,y)/f)R_i(x):X_f(x)=1\\}\n\\mid Y_f(y)=1\n\\right],\n\\]\n\\[\n\\widehat g_i(y)=\\gamma\\!\\left(\\max_{r\\le i}U_r(y)\\right),\n\\quad\n\\widehat E_i(x,y)=fQ(G_i(x,y)/f)\\widehat g_i(y).\n\\]\nRanks have unlimited zero padding.</p><p>Set \\(g=\\widehat g_{k_*}\\), use this fixed multiplier in Lemma 2.1's recurrence, and put\n\\[\nE=E_{k_*},\\qquad D=C-E+EC.\n\\]</p><p>For \\(1\\le i\\le k_*\\), all these inputs and corrections are nonnegative and strictly forward,\n\\[\nG_i\\mathbf1,\\widehat E_i\\mathbf1,G_i'\\mathbf1,E_i\\mathbf1\\le i\\mathbf1,\n\\]\n\\[\nG_i(x,y),\\widehat E_i(x,y),G_i'(x,y),E_i(x,y)\\le if,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nE_{i-1}\\le E_i\\le\\widehat E_i,\\qquad G_i'\\le G_i.\n\\]\nTheir row and column supports are contained in those of \\(C\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The cutoff gives \\(Q(0)=0\\) and \\(0\\le Q(t)\\le t\\), so \\(\\widehat E_i\\le G_i\\). If \\(\\widehat E_{i-1}\\mathbf1\\le(i-1)\\mathbf1\\), then\n\\[\nG_i\\mathbf1\\le\\mathbf1+\\widehat E_{i-1}\\mathbf1\\le i\\mathbf1,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nG_i(x,y)\\le f+f\\sum_z\\widehat E_{i-1}(x,z)\\le if.\n\\]\nThis proves the pilot bounds.</p><p>The multipliers \\(\\widehat g_i\\) are nonincreasing, so \\(g\\le\\widehat g_i\\). Induction and monotonicity of \\(Q\\) give \\(E_i\\le\\widehat E_i\\) and \\(G_i'\\le G_i\\). The fixed-multiplier update is monotone, so its iteration from zero is nondecreasing.</p><p>Products of strictly forward matrices are strictly forward. A zero row of \\(C\\) remains zero throughout the recurrence, and a zero column remains zero in every product \\(FC\\). This proves the support conclusions.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.3", "orig": "Lemma 4.3, “The detector bounds overloaded columns.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Under Lemma 2.2's definitions, let \\( D_{\\mathrm{row}}=1+C_{\\mathrm{pair}}k_*/\\eta_-. \\) Choose \\(K\\ge160D_{\\mathrm{row}}\\), enlarged for the finitely many other row-gate estimates.", "statement": "<p>Under Lemma 2.2's definitions, let\n\\[\nD_{\\mathrm{row}}=1+C_{\\mathrm{pair}}k_*/\\eta_-.\n\\]\nChoose \\(K\\ge160D_{\\mathrm{row}}\\), enlarged for the finitely many other row-gate estimates. For any fixed positive integer \\(J\\), choose\n\\[\nR_*\\ge c_\\pi^{-1}\\max(4J,40C_{\\mathrm{var}}).\n\\]\nThen:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. If \\(\\#\\{x:G_i(x,y)\\ge\\delta_1f\\}&gt;R_*/f\\), then \\(U_i(y)\\ge19/20\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. If \\(U_i(y)&gt;1/4\\), then\n\\[\n\\#\\{x:G_i(x,y)&gt;\\delta_0f\\}&gt;\\frac{J}{4C_\\pi f}.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li num\">3. If \\(\\widehat g_i(y)&gt;0\\), then the high-entry count in item 1 is at most \\(R_*/f\\). If \\(g(y)&gt;0\\), the same bound holds for every \\(G_i'\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix \\(i,y\\) and condition on \\(Y_f(y)=1\\). By the row-mass bound, at most \\(k_*/(\\eta_-f)\\) columns have \\(G_i(x,w)&gt;\\eta_-f\\). Their expected retained count is at most \\(D_{\\mathrm{row}}\\): the forced endpoint contributes at most one, and every other endpoint uses the conditional-pair bound. Thus\n\\[\n\\Pr[R_i(x)&lt;1\\mid Y_f(y)=1]\\le D_{\\mathrm{row}}/K.\n\\]</p><p>Let \\(A=\\{x:G_i(x,y)\\ge\\delta_1f\\}\\), \\(n=|A|\\). Let \\(N_A\\) count its retained starts and \\(L_A\\) count retained starts with nonfull row gates. Independence of \\(X,Y\\) gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb E[L_A\\mid Y_f(y)=1]\\le n\\pi_fD_{\\mathrm{row}}/K.\n\\]\nHence \\(\\Pr[L_A&gt;n\\pi_f/4\\mid Y_f(y)=1]\\le1/40\\). The count estimate gives\n\\[\n\\Pr[N_A&lt;n\\pi_f/2\\mid Y_f(y)=1]\\le C_{\\mathrm{var}}/(n\\pi_f).\n\\]\nWhen \\(n&gt;R_*/f\\), outside an event of probability at most \\(1/20\\), at least \\(J\\) retained high rows have full gates. Their scores are one, so the trial rank is one. This proves item 1 without independence among row-gate failures.</p><p>For \\(A_0=\\{x:G_i(x,y)&gt;\\delta_0f\\}\\), a positive trial requires at least \\(J\\) retained elements of \\(A_0\\). Pointwise the trial is at most their count divided by \\(J\\), and its mean is at most \\(|A_0|C_\\pi f/J\\). This proves item 2.</p><p>A positive multiplier implies the relevant detector value is below \\(4/5\\), contradicting item 1 if the high-entry count were too large. For actual inputs also use \\(G_i'\\le G_i\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.4", "orig": "Lemma 4.4, “Residual bounds and unsigned-term domination.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Under Lemma 2.2's definitions, put \\( K_*=k_*+1,\\qquad \\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{good}}=\\theta_2+q^{-1}/32<q^{-1}/16, \\qquad c_{\\mathrm{dom}}=\\theta_1/(2k_*). \\) Then \\(D=C-E+EC\\) is nonnegative, strictly forward, and …", "statement": "<p>Under Lemma 2.2's definitions, put\n\\[\nK_*=k_*+1,\\qquad\n\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{good}}=\\theta_2+q^{-1}/32&lt;q^{-1}/16,\n\\qquad c_{\\mathrm{dom}}=\\theta_1/(2k_*).\n\\]\nThen \\(D=C-E+EC\\) is nonnegative, strictly forward, and substochastic, with\n\\[\nD(x,y)\\le K_*f,\\qquad\ng(y)=1\\Longrightarrow D(x,y)\\le\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{good}}f,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nD\\ge c_{\\mathrm{dom}}(C+E+EC).\n\\]\nEach unsigned summand in \\(G_i=C+\\widehat E_{i-1}C\\) and \\(G_i'=C+E_{i-1}C\\) is bounded by that input. Also \\(R(D)\\subseteq R(C)\\), \\(T(D)\\subseteq T(C)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Set \\(A=G_{k_*}'\\) and \\(\\delta=(E-E_{k_*-1})C\\ge0\\). Then\n\\[\nD=A-E+\\delta,\\qquad A-E\\ge\\min(A,\\theta_1f),\n\\]\nso \\(D\\ge0\\). Moreover,\n\\[\nD\\mathbf1=C\\mathbf1-E(\\mathbf1-C\\mathbf1)\\le\\mathbf1,\n\\qquad D\\le C+EC,\n\\]\nand the latter has entries at most \\((k_*+1)f\\).</p><p>Since \\(A(x,y)\\le k_*f\\),\n\\[\nA-E\\ge(\\theta_1/k_*)A,\\qquad\nC+E+EC=A+E+\\delta\\le2A+\\delta.\n\\]\nThese imply unsigned-term domination.</p><p>For a good column, compare with the fixed point for the same \\(g\\). With \\(\\Delta=E_*-E\\) and \\(T=C-E_*+E_*C\\),\n\\[\nD=T+\\Delta-\\Delta C\\le T+\\Delta.\n\\]\nIf \\(g(y)=1\\), the lower cutoff bound gives \\(T(x,y)\\le\\theta_2f\\), and Lemma 2.1 gives \\(\\Delta(x,y)\\le q^{-1}f/32\\). Strict forwardness and support containment follow from the defining formulas.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.5", "orig": "Proposition 4.5, “One-stage density and active-count reduction.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Under the preceding hierarchy definitions, choose \\( D_0>K_*q,\\qquad \\tau_0=q^{-1}/8,\\quad \\tau_1=q^{-1}/4. \\) Let \\(\\chi\\) rise from zero through \\(\\tau_0\\) to one from \\(\\tau_1\\), and define …", "statement": "<p>Under the preceding hierarchy definitions, choose\n\\[\nD_0&gt;K_*q,\\qquad \\tau_0=q^{-1}/8,\\quad \\tau_1=q^{-1}/4.\n\\]\nLet \\(\\chi\\) rise from zero through \\(\\tau_0\\) to one from \\(\\tau_1\\), and define\n\\[\ns_0(t)=1-(1-D_0^{-1})\\chi(t),\\qquad\ns_j(t)=D_0^{-1}\\chi(t)\\quad(j&gt;0).\n\\]\nLet \\(U^{\\max}(x)=\\max_{i\\le k_*}U_i(x)\\), let \\(\\kappa\\) rise from zero through \\(1/4\\) to one from \\(1/2\\), and set\n\\[\nr_0(x)=1,\\qquad r_j(x)=\\kappa(U^{\\max}(x))\\quad(j&gt;0).\n\\]\nOn \\(V^+=V\\times\\{0,\\ldots,D_0-1\\}\\), ordered first by parent, put\n\\[\nC^+(x_i,y_j)=r_i(x)D(x,y)s_j(D(x,y)/f),\n\\]\nand define \\(P^r(x_i,x)=r_i(x)\\), zero elsewhere.</p><p>If\n\\[\nJ\\ge\\frac{2C_\\pi(D_0-1)k_*(k_*+1)}{\\delta_0},\n\\]\nand \\(R_*\\) is subsequently chosen as in Lemma 2.3, then\n\\[\nC^+\\mathbf1\\le\\mathbf1,\\quad\nC^+(x_i,y_j)\\le f/q,\\quad\n|A(C^+)|\\le2|A(C)|,\\quad\nC^+P^r=P^rD.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The row sum is \\(r_i(x)\\sum_yD(x,y)\\le1\\). Strict forwardness follows from \\(D\\). If \\(t=D(x,y)/f&lt;\\tau_1\\), every routed entry is below \\(f/q\\). If \\(t\\ge\\tau_1\\), all routing fractions equal \\(1/D_0\\), so every entry is at most \\(K_*f/D_0&lt;f/q\\).</p><p>A positive nonprimary fraction requires\n\\[\nD(x,y)/f&gt;\\tau_0&gt;\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{good}}.\n\\]\nHence \\(g(y)&lt;1\\), so \\(U^{\\max}(y)&gt;1/2\\) and all nonprimary \\(r_j(y)=1\\). Thus, at every positive entry,\n\\[\n\\sum_j s_j(D(x,y)/f)r_j(y)=1.\n\\]\nMultiplying by \\(P^r\\) proves the intertwining identity.</p><p>Let \\(O=\\{y:U^{\\max}(y)&gt;1/4\\}\\) and \\(n'=|A(C)|\\). Each \\(y\\in O\\) has more than \\(J/(4C_\\pi f)\\) substantial entries at some pilot step. Counting incidences and using the pilot row-mass bounds,\n\\[\n|O|\\frac{J}{4C_\\pi f}\n\\le\\sum_{i=1}^{k_*}\\#\\{(x,y):G_i(x,y)&gt;\\delta_0f\\}\n\\le\\frac{n'k_*(k_*+1)}{2\\delta_0f}.\n\\]\nEvery active child has an active parent. A nonprimary active row has \\(r_j&gt;0\\), and a nonprimary active column has a positive extra routing fraction; either parent belongs to \\(O\\). Therefore\n\\[\n|A(C^+)|\\le n'+(D_0-1)|O|\\le2n'.\n\\]</p><p>The constants are chosen in the paper's order: \\(\\theta_1,\\theta_2,Q,k_*,K_*,D_0\\), then threshold ladders and \\(K\\), then \\(J,R_*\\), and only afterward the incoming rank. No enlargement of \\(K\\) in response to the incoming rank is required.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.6", "orig": "Proposition 4.7, “Reward identity and a shallow target.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Starting from \\(C_0=S\\), repeat Lemma 2.5 at rates \\(f_l=q^{-l}\\), with \\( V_{l+1}=V_l\\times\\{0,\\ldots,D_0-1\\}. \\) Let \\(E_l,D_l,P_l^r\\) be the stage correction, residual, and projection.", "statement": "<p>Starting from \\(C_0=S\\), repeat Lemma 2.5 at rates \\(f_l=q^{-l}\\), with\n\\[\nV_{l+1}=V_l\\times\\{0,\\ldots,D_0-1\\}.\n\\]\nLet \\(E_l,D_l,P_l^r\\) be the stage correction, residual, and projection. Define\n\\[\nP_0^c=I,\\quad P_{l+1}^c=P_l^rP_l^c,\\qquad\nB_0=I,\\quad B_{l+1}=P_l^r(I+E_l)B_l.\n\\]\nThe superscript \\(c\\) denotes cumulative projection, not transpose.</p><p>For \\(p_0=(I-S)^{-1}e\\) and \\(W_l=B_le\\),\n\\[\nC_lP_l^c=P_l^c-B_l(I-S),\n\\]\n\\[\n0\\le W_l\\le1,\\qquad\nW_l=P_l^cp_0-C_lP_l^cp_0,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nW_{l+1}(x_i)=r_i(x)\\bigl(W_l(x)+(E_lW_l)(x)\\bigr).\n\\]\nAt an all-primary copy,\n\\[\n0\\le p_0(x)-W_l(\\bar x_l)\\le |V_0|2^{-(H-1)l}.\n\\]\nIn particular, some \\(L=O(B)\\) makes this bound strictly below \\(1/16\\) for every base vertex; then \\(p_0(x)=0\\) implies \\(W_L(\\bar x_L)=0\\), and \\(p_0(x)\\ge1/2\\) implies \\(W_L(\\bar x_L)&gt;7/16\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The identity at stage zero is \\(S=I-(I-S)\\). Since\n\\[\nD_l=I-(I+E_l)(I-C_l),\n\\]\nthe intertwining relation and induction give\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nC_{l+1}P_{l+1}^c\n&amp;=P_l^rD_lP_l^c\\\\\n&amp;=P_l^r\\bigl(P_l^c-(I+E_l)(P_l^c-C_lP_l^c)\\bigr)\\\\\n&amp;=P_{l+1}^c-B_{l+1}(I-S).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nApply this to \\(p_0\\). Every \\(B_l\\) is nonnegative, and every cumulative projection is substochastic, so \\(0\\le W_l\\le P_l^cp_0\\le1\\). The update follows from the definition of \\(B_{l+1}\\).</p><p>Induction gives\n\\[\n|A(C_l)|\\le2^l|V_0|,\\qquad C_l(x,y)\\le q^{-l}.\n\\]\nThus every row sum is at most \\(|V_0|2^{-(H-1)l}\\). The all-primary projection has all factors equal to one. Using \\(0\\le P_l^cp_0\\le1\\) proves the error bound. Choose\n\\[\n(H-1)L&gt;\\log(16|V_0|).\n\\]\nFinally, \\(|V_l|=|V_0|D_0^l=2^{O(B)}\\), and parent projection deletes one copy symbol.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The hierarchy contributions admit bounded stochastic row tables with exact conditional-mean identities, bounded row supports, and smooth support witnesses.", "statement": "<p>Stochastic atoms, support, and assembly interfaces.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix a hierarchy stage of Proposition 2 with rate \\(f\\), and put \\(s=8\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A raw input \\(G\\ge0\\) satisfies\n\\[\nG\\mathbf1\\le M_G\\mathbf1,\\qquad G(x,y)\\le K_Gf\n\\]\nfor fixed constants. For a correction \\(F=fQ(G/f)g_0\\), assume\n\\[\ng_0(y)&gt;0\\Longrightarrow\n\\#\\{x:G(x,y)\\ge\\delta_1f\\}\\le R_*/f.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">A row task has start rate \\(u\\le f\\), endpoint scale \\(a\\le K_ff\\), and optional usage rate \\(t\\le a\\), all on the rate grid. Its broad domain is \\(X_u(x)=Y_a(y)=1\\), using distinct channels.</p><p class=\"li\">Its true first raw signal is \\(d_y=G(x,y)/a\\). An optional second signal is \\(\\zeta1_{\\{Y_t(y)=1\\}}q_y\\), where \\(q_y\\in[0,1]\\) is the specified deterministic preparatory conditional mean.</p><p class=\"li\">Task parameters—stage, iteration, type, channel roles, suffixes, rates, budget, and numerical convention—are common to the entire table. Row and column identifiers are arguments, not extra tuple parameters.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\operatorname{rk}_k\\) means the \\(k\\)-th largest entry with zero padding.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\chi_{\\alpha,\\beta}\\) be the increasing switch obtained from the normalized integral of \\(z^{s-1}(1-z)^{s-1}\\), zero through \\(\\alpha\\) and one from \\(\\beta\\). It and its complement have Lipschitz \\(s\\)-th roots.</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n\\[\nh_\\vartheta(v)=\\chi_{\\vartheta/2,\\,3\\vartheta/4}(v),\\quad\nR_\\vartheta(\\rho)=1-\\chi_{\\vartheta/1000,\\,\\vartheta/500}(\\rho),\n\\]\n\\[\nI_\\vartheta(v,\\rho)=\n\\chi_{\\vartheta/8,\\,\\vartheta/4}(v)\n\\bigl(1-\\chi_{\\vartheta/100,\\,\\vartheta/50}(\\rho)\\bigr).\n\\]\nUse \\(\\vartheta=1/2\\) for bins, \\(\\theta_1\\) for corrections, and \\(\\delta_0\\) for detector scores; choose the hierarchy detector gate to equal \\(R_{\\delta_0}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(c(q)=\\min(1,\\max(1/2,q))\\). Scalar functions have the prescribed zero extensions and range clips; ranks and access tests use the full raw arrays.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The hierarchy contributions admit bounded stochastic row tables with exact conditional-mean identities, bounded row supports, and smooth support witnesses. Correction tables have capped exposed relations with bounded incoming and outgoing degree. These support and access statements hold also for arbitrary computed raw arrays, provided each version uses one consistent final array and tuple. Their assembly discrepancies obey Lemma 3.7.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas 3.1 and 3.3 give exact normalized atom means; Lemma 3.2 bounds fine product contributions; Lemma 3.6 identifies detector trials. Lemmas 3.4–3.5 give the support, witness, and exposed-port contracts even for inaccurate arrays, and Lemma 3.7 controls assembly.</p><p>All required pre-incoming functions have entry threshold \\(e_H\\) and gate-closing rank \\(g_H\\) with \\(3g_H&lt;e_H\\). The pairs are\n\\[\n(1/2,1/1000),\\ (1/4,1/1000),\\\n(\\theta_1,\\theta_1/500),\\\n(\\theta_1/2,\\theta_1/500),\\\n(\\delta_0,\\delta_0/500),\\\n(\\vartheta/8,\\vartheta/50),\\\n(82\\vartheta/1000,27\\vartheta/1000).\n\\]\nThey correspond respectively to bins, dense preparations, correction ratios, correction preparations, detector scores, auxiliary scores, and witnesses. Their roots are bounded and Lipschitz on fixed sensitivity ranges. Choose \\(\\zeta\\) sufficiently small relative to these thresholds. Dividing the computed second signal by \\(\\zeta\\) and applying \\(c\\) keeps every denominator in \\([1/2,1]\\), regardless of accuracy.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.1", "orig": "Lemma 5.1, “Dense atom identity.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(q_*=2^H\\), \\(\\chi=\\chi_{1/2,1}\\), and \\( \\omega_0(w)=\\chi(w),\\qquad \\omega_k(w)=\\chi(w/b_k)-\\chi(w/b_{k-1})\\quad(k\\ge1). \\) For \\(a=b_k\\), put \\( k_a(d)=d\\omega_k(ad),\\qquad k_a^{(j)}(d)=k_a(d)s_j(ad/f). \\) For …", "statement": "<p>Let \\(q_*=2^H\\), \\(\\chi=\\chi_{1/2,1}\\), and\n\\[\n\\omega_0(w)=\\chi(w),\\qquad\n\\omega_k(w)=\\chi(w/b_k)-\\chi(w/b_{k-1})\\quad(k\\ge1).\n\\]\nFor \\(a=b_k\\), put\n\\[\nk_a(d)=d\\omega_k(ad),\\qquad\nk_a^{(j)}(d)=k_a(d)s_j(ad/f).\n\\]\nFor \\(k=0\\), evaluate the expression at \\(\\operatorname{clip}_{[0,B_{\\mathrm{top}}]}d\\), where \\(B_{\\mathrm{top}}&gt;\\max(K_G,1)\\). For \\(k\\ge1\\), use its zero extension outside \\((1/2,q_*)\\).</p><p>Let\n\\[\n\\rho_x=\\operatorname{rk}_K\\{G(x,z)/a:Y_a(z)=1\\},\\quad\nR=R_{1/2}(\\rho_x),\n\\]\n\\[\nq_y=\\mathbb E[h_{1/2}(d_y)R\\mid X_u(x)=Y_t(y)=1].\n\\]\nFor sufficiently large fixed \\(K\\), the table\n\\[\n\\mathcal B_a(x,y)=1_{\\{Y_t(y)=1\\}}\\frac{k_a(d_y)R}{c(q_y)}\n\\]\nhas bounded values and row support. On \\(X_u(x)=Y_t(y)=1\\), its conditional expectation multiplied by \\(a\\) is \\(G(x,y)\\omega_k(G(x,y))\\). The same holds with \\(k_a^{(j)}\\). Apart from start membership, its true value uses only \\(Y\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The weights are nonnegative and telescope to one for \\(w&gt;0\\), so\n\\[\nw=\\sum_{k\\ge0}w\\omega_k(w).\n\\]\nFor \\(k\\ge1\\), the bin support is \\(1/2&lt;d&lt;q_*\\). Its switch intervals are disjoint, and\n\\[\n\\chi(d)-\\chi(d/q_*)=\\chi(d)(1-\\chi(d/q_*)),\n\\]\ngiving a Lipschitz root. The top clipping preserves true values. Bins with \\(a&gt;2K_Gf\\) vanish; choose \\(K_f\\ge2K_G\\). Routed fractions preserve the root regularity because \\(a/f\\le K_f\\).</p><p>A nonfull row gate requires at least \\(K\\) sampled columns with \\(G(x,z)/a&gt;r\\), where \\(r=(1/2)/1000\\). There are at most \\(M_G/(ar)\\) deterministic such columns. Conditional on the endpoint hit, their expected retained count is at most \\(1+CM_G/r\\), by the pair bound. The independent start condition does not change this. For large \\(K\\), the failure probability is at most \\(1/8\\).</p><p>On \\(k_a(d_y)&gt;0\\), \\(h_{1/2}(d_y)=1\\), so \\(q_y=\\mathbb E[R\\mid Y_t(y)=1]\\in[7/8,1]\\). Denominator clipping does nothing, and division gives the asserted mean. Outside bin support both sides vanish. A positive output requires first entry \\(&gt;1/2\\) and \\(K\\)-th rank \\(&lt;1/1000\\), so there are at most \\(K-1\\) positive entries. The denominator bounds the range. The routed-bin argument is identical because its extra factor is deterministic at truth.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.2", "orig": "Lemma 5.2, “Bounds for a fine product contribution.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(C\\) be the current transition and \\(F\\) any finite pilot or actual correction.", "statement": "<p>Let \\(C\\) be the current transition and \\(F\\) any finite pilot or actual correction. If \\(A_b\\ge0\\) satisfies\n\\[\nA_b\\mathbf1\\le\\mathbf1,\\qquad A_b(x,y)\\le c_bb,\n\\]\nthen\n\\[\n(FA_b)\\mathbf1\\le k_*\\mathbf1,\\qquad\n(FA_b)(x,y)\\le k_*c_bb.\n\\]\nIf \\(A_b\\le C\\), then \\(FA_b\\le FC\\). Constants are independent of the bin scale \\(b\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Use \\(F\\mathbf1\\le k_*\\mathbf1\\):\n\\[\n(FA_b)\\mathbf1\\le F\\mathbf1,\\qquad\n(FA_b)(x,y)\\le c_bb\\sum_zF(x,z).\n\\]\nMultiplication by nonnegative \\(F\\) preserves \\(A_b\\le C\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.3", "orig": "Lemma 5.3, “Correction atom identity, including zero multipliers.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For \\(F=fQ(G/f)g_0(y)\\), use \\(a=f\\), \\(\\vartheta=\\theta_1\\), and \\( Q_{\\mathrm{at}}(d)=Q(\\operatorname{clip}_{[0,B_Q]}d), \\qquad B_Q>\\max(K_G,\\theta_2). \\) Let \\(R=R_{\\theta_1}(\\rho_x)\\), \\(h=h_{\\theta_1}\\), and let …", "statement": "<p>For \\(F=fQ(G/f)g_0(y)\\), use \\(a=f\\), \\(\\vartheta=\\theta_1\\), and\n\\[\nQ_{\\mathrm{at}}(d)=Q(\\operatorname{clip}_{[0,B_Q]}d),\n\\qquad B_Q&gt;\\max(K_G,\\theta_2).\n\\]\nLet \\(R=R_{\\theta_1}(\\rho_x)\\), \\(h=h_{\\theta_1}\\), and let \\(I(x,y)\\) be \\(I_{\\theta_1}\\) applied to this table's own entry and row rank. Define\n\\[\nT_y=1-\\chi_{1/4,1/2}\n\\left(\\operatorname{rk}_{K_c}\\{I(x,y):X_u(x)=1\\}\\right),\n\\]\n\\[\nq_y=\\mathbb E[h(d_y)RT_y\\mid X_u(x)=Y_t(y)=1],\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathcal F(x,y)=\n1_{\\{Y_t(y)=1\\}}\n\\frac{Q_{\\mathrm{at}}(d_y)RT_y}{c(q_y)}g_0(y).\n\\]\nThe incoming rank is over the fixed retained source set before port filtering.</p><p>For sufficiently large \\(K\\), then \\(K_c\\), the conditional mean of \\(f\\mathcal F(x,y)\\) is \\(F(x,y)\\). On a true position with \\(Q(d_y)&gt;0\\) and \\(g_0(y)&gt;0\\),\n\\[\nq_y=\\mathbb E[RT_y\\mid X_u(x)=Y_t(y)=1]\\ge3/4.\n\\]\nNo lower bound is required when \\(g_0(y)=0\\). The true gates and normalization use only \\(X,Y\\); \\(g_0\\) is deterministic.</p>", "proof": "<p>The row-count argument gives \\(\\Pr[R\\ne1\\mid\\text{two hits}]\\le1/8\\). If \\(g_0(y)&gt;0\\), every source with \\(I(x',y)&gt;0\\) lies among the at most \\(R_*/f\\) substantial sources. Conditional on \\(X_u(x)=1\\), their expected retained count is at most\n\\[\n1+CuR_*/f\\le1+CR_*.\n\\]\nConditioning on the independent terminal hit leaves this bound valid; no independence among auxiliary row gates is needed. Choose \\(K_c\\ge8(1+CR_*)\\). Then \\(\\Pr[T_y\\ne1\\mid\\text{two hits}]\\le1/8\\), and the union bound gives the mean lower bound \\(3/4\\).</p><p>On \\(Q\\)'s support, \\(h=1\\). If also \\(g_0&gt;0\\), clipping leaves \\(q_y\\) unchanged, proving the identity by normalization. If \\(Q=0\\) or \\(g_0=0\\), both sides are zero. The pre-incoming clipped expression remains bounded even when the unclipped mean vanishes. Also \\(q_y=0\\) for \\(d_y\\le\\theta_1/2\\), as required for the second signal.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.4", "orig": "Lemma 5.5, “Support margins and exposed row classes.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(k(v)\\) be bounded, nonnegative, and zero for \\(v\\le\\vartheta\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(k(v)\\) be bounded, nonnegative, and zero for \\(v\\le\\vartheta\\). For an arbitrary real raw row \\(v\\), let \\(\\rho=\\operatorname{rk}_K(v)\\). The products \\(k(v)R_\\vartheta(\\rho)\\) and \\(h_\\vartheta(v)R_\\vartheta(\\rho)\\) have at most \\(K-1\\) nonzero real entries.</p><p>Choose repeatable approximations \\(\\widetilde v,\\widetilde\\rho\\) with absolute errors at most \\(\\vartheta/10000\\), and define\n\\[\nA=\\{y:Y_a(y)=1,\\ \\widetilde v_y&gt;3\\vartheta/8,\\\n\\widetilde\\rho&lt;\\vartheta/200\\}.\n\\]\nThis class has at most \\(K-1\\) entries, contains both supports, and has \\(I_\\vartheta=1\\) throughout.</p><p>The score \\(I_\\vartheta\\) also has at most \\(K-1\\) nonzero entries. Its exposed class\n\\[\nA_I=\\{y:Y_a(y)=1,\\ \\widetilde v_y&gt;\\vartheta/10,\\\n\\widetilde\\rho&lt;\\vartheta/40\\}\n\\]\nhas a bounded-support smooth unit witness\n\\[\nJ_\\vartheta(v,\\rho)=\n\\chi_{82\\vartheta/1000,\\,90\\vartheta/1000}(v)\n\\bigl(1-\\chi_{26\\vartheta/1000,\\,27\\vartheta/1000}(\\rho)\\bigr).\n\\]\nThe witness requires no further recursively broadened access class.</p>", "proof": "<p>A nonzero main or preparation entry requires \\(v&gt;\\vartheta/2\\) and \\(\\rho&lt;\\vartheta/500\\); \\(K\\) such entries would contradict the rank bound. The approximation margins put every such entry in \\(A\\), and imply \\(v&gt;\\vartheta/4\\), \\(\\rho&lt;\\vartheta/100\\) on \\(A\\), so \\(I_\\vartheta=1\\). The entry threshold exceeds the rank threshold, proving \\(|A|\\le K-1\\).</p><p>The support of \\(I_\\vartheta\\) requires \\(v&gt;\\vartheta/8\\), \\(\\rho&lt;\\vartheta/50\\), giving the same cap. The margins show that \\(A_I\\) contains this support and lies where \\(J_\\vartheta=1\\). The separation\n\\[\n82/1000&gt;3(27/1000)\n\\]\ngives its bounded support and compression margin.</p><p>Ranks include arbitrary dummy copies of the common default. If that default is \\(\\beta\\), then \\(\\rho\\ge\\beta\\), so a default-valued real position cannot satisfy an exposed class's larger entry and smaller rank thresholds. Thus omitted default positions create no unlisted ports.</p><p>The approximations are fixed-accuracy access conventions, not exact-real equality tests. Each table must use them consistently.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.5", "orig": "Lemma 5.6, “Port contract.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Under Proposition 3's row functions and Lemma 3.4's classes:", "statement": "<p>Under Proposition 3's row functions and Lemma 3.4's classes:</p><p class=\"li\">Main and preparation outputs, auxiliary scores, and witnesses have bounded values and row supports.</p><p class=\"li\">Every exposed row class has bounded size and a smooth witness equal to one at all exposed entries, including zero-weight entries.</p><p class=\"li\">For a correction or correction preparation, retain \\((x,y)\\in A\\) only if\n\\[\n\\#\\{x':X_u(x')=1,\\ (x',y)\\in A\\}\\le K_c.\n\\]\nThis preserves values and gives at most \\(K-1\\) outgoing and \\(K_c\\) incoming exposed entries.</p><p class=\"li\">Ports denote distinct ID pairs, not paths. Each pair is exposed at most once, with consistently paired incoming and outgoing ports.</p><p class=\"li\">These statements hold separately for arbitrary computed versions using their own consistent final raw arrays and common tuples.</p><p>Only completed earlier output types, stated fixed local functions, and aggregations of such outputs may serve as later raw inputs. A prefix waiting for assembly is not a completed output.</p>", "proof": "<p>The row bounds and witnesses follow from Lemma 3.4. Bounded clipping and multiplication do not enlarge supports. If a column has more than \\(K_c\\) entries in \\(A\\), its auxiliary score is one at all of them. Its \\(K_c\\)-th rank is one, so \\(T_y=0\\). Every deleted value was already zero.</p><p>The cap deletes every precursor port in an overfull column; it does not select \\(K_c\\) of them. It applies even to zero-weight ports. A surviving precursor remains in its unit witness regardless of usage mask, incoming gate, or multiplier. Auxiliary score access uses the broader uncapped class \\(A_I\\), requiring only its row bound.</p><p>A finite list and common default represent one full raw array. Its \\(k\\)-th rank is computed from the listed coordinates and \\(k\\) default copies; extra listed entries equal to default do not change that rank. A common componentwise clamp preserves this representation. This is the content of original Remark 5.4.</p><p>Values, scores, gates, ranks, and multipliers are all evaluated at the same environment and tuple. Preparations are earlier separate tasks; a normalized task uses its computed second raw signal and does not launch another averaging experiment after endpoint selection. Auxiliary scores and precursor classes precede their own incoming gate and cap, so the definitions are acyclic. Enumeration and pairing remain implementation obligations rather than consequences of degree bounds alone.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.6", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Detector table and its original conditional-mean specification, Section 5.4.", "statement": "<p>Detector table and its original conditional-mean specification, Section 5.4.</p><p>Under the pilot definitions of Proposition 2, set\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_i(x,y)=\n\\varphi(G_i(x,y)/f)\\,\nR_{\\delta_0}\\!\\left(\n\\operatorname{rk}_K\\{G_i(x,w)/f:Y_f(w)=1\\}\\right)\n\\]\non \\(X_f(x)=Y_f(y)=1\\).</p><p>The conditional mean, given \\(Y_f(y)=1\\), of\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}_J\\{\\mathcal S_i(x,y):X_f(x)=1\\}\n\\]\nis exactly \\(U_i(y)\\). This is an unnormalized row-bounded score table; the column trial does not require an incoming-degree cap.</p>", "proof": "<p>The chosen detector gate is exactly the hierarchy's \\(R_i\\). Substitution into the defining formula for \\(U_i\\) gives the identity. The detector entry and gate thresholds are \\(\\delta_0\\) and \\(\\delta_0/500\\), so the support argument of Lemma 3.4 applies.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.7", "orig": "Lemma 5.7, “Assembly comparisons.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For row arrays define \\( \\|Z\\|_{u,a;s}^s= \\frac1N\\mathbb E_\\sigma\\sum_x\\frac{X_u(x)}u \\sum_{y:Y_a(y)=1}|Z(x,y)|^s, \\quad d_{u,a}(H,H')=\\|H^{1/s}-(H')^{1/s}\\|_{u,a;s}. \\) For a vertex array on rate \\(u\\), define …", "statement": "<p>For row arrays define\n\\[\n\\|Z\\|_{u,a;s}^s=\n\\frac1N\\mathbb E_\\sigma\\sum_x\\frac{X_u(x)}u\n\\sum_{y:Y_a(y)=1}|Z(x,y)|^s,\n\\quad\nd_{u,a}(H,H')=\\|H^{1/s}-(H')^{1/s}\\|_{u,a;s}.\n\\]\nFor a vertex array on rate \\(u\\), define\n\\[\n\\|v\\|_{u;s}^s=\n\\frac1N\\mathbb E_\\sigma\\sum_x\\frac{X_u(x)}u|v(x)|^s.\n\\]\nThe indicated role channel replaces \\(X\\) as necessary; all tables are zero off their domains.</p><p>Compare two versions with the same environment and tuple. Write a sparse normalized output as \\(H=PTg\\), \\(H'=P'T'g'\\), with auxiliary scores \\(I,I'\\), and let \\(g,g'\\) be the prescribed cutoffs of bounded detector vectors \\(U_i,U_i'\\). Then\n\\[\nd_{u,f}(H,H')^s\n\\le C d_{u,f}(P,P')^s+\nC d_{u,f}(I,I')^s+\nC\\frac fu\\sum_i\\|U_i-U_i'\\|_{f;s}^s.\n\\]\nFor sparse preparations omit the last term. Dense and detector rows have no additional incoming or multiplier cost.</p><p>For detector trials \\(V,V'\\),\n\\[\n\\|V-V'\\|_{f;s}^s\\le C d_{f,f}(\\mathcal S_i,\\mathcal S_i')^s.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>On bounded nonnegative values,\n\\[\n|z-w|\\le sC^{(s-1)/s}|z^{1/s}-w^{1/s}|.\n\\]\nSplit the product-root difference on the union of the final supports. The pre-incoming discrepancy is charged over its full row domain. Each column of this union contains at most \\(2K_c\\) edges.</p><p>Order statistics are sup-norm Lipschitz:\n\\[\n|\\operatorname{rk}_k(v)-\\operatorname{rk}_k(w)|\n\\le\\sup_z|v_z-w_z|.\n\\]\nThe incoming cutoff's root regularity therefore bounds its discrepancy by a supremum of auxiliary-score discrepancies, whose \\(s\\)-th power is at most the corresponding sum. Reversing the row and column sums gives \\(Cd_{u,f}(I,I')^s\\).</p><p>The multiplier discrepancy is bounded by \\(C\\sum_i|U_i(y)-U_i'(y)|^s\\). Summing over at most \\(2K_c\\) incoming edges per column changes the vertex weight \\(1/f\\) to \\(1/u\\), producing exactly \\(f/u\\). The common tuple ensures that every old source sees the same vector discrepancy at \\(y\\); no independence is used.</p><p>The detector trial discrepancy is at most the supremum of score discrepancies over retained starts. Summing its power and reversing the two sums gives the second assertion at \\(u=a=f\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is a rational conditional averaging operator \\(K\\), stationary for this uniform conditional law, with \\( \\|K^d-\\Pi\\|_{L^s\\to L^s}\\le C_s2^{-c_sd} \\qquad(2\\le s<\\infty). \\) A length-\\(d\\) walk has …", "statement": "<p>Conditional averaging and endpoint-independent validated variants.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use a fixed product of rank channels from Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Condition on one specified retained identifier in each conditioned channel.</p><p class=\"li\">A designated set of independent uniform bits, padded to length divisible by \\(H\\), is free; the remaining bits are frozen.</p><p class=\"li\">The conditional law includes the frame restrictions, retention conditions, and fixed frozen bits.</p><p class=\"li\">The external group-theoretic inputs are property \\((T)\\) of \\(\\mathrm{SL}_H(\\mathbb Z)\\) for \\(H\\ge3\\) and relative property \\((T)\\) of\n\\[\n(\\mathrm{SL}_2(\\mathbb Z)\\ltimes\\mathbb Z^2,\\mathbb Z^2),\n\\]\nin the quantitative forms cited from Shalom.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a rational conditional averaging operator \\(K\\), stationary for this uniform conditional law, with\n\\[\n\\|K^d-\\Pi\\|_{L^s\\to L^s}\\le C_s2^{-c_sd}\n\\qquad(2\\le s&lt;\\infty).\n\\]\nA length-\\(d\\) walk has endpoint-independent, full-environment measure-preserving variants described by \\(O(d)\\) bits. Unique validation on the indicated slice recovers exactly the conditional walk with the original walk-word weights.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas 4.1–4.2 give uniform slice mixing, and Lemma 4.3 supplies its endpoint-independent representation.</p><p>Two uses remain distinct. Centered-noise analysis first sums valid variants into the conditional walk and then applies the spectral estimate. Crude comparisons instead change variables by each fixed full-environment bijection and may pay their \\(2^{O(d)}\\) count. Free and frozen bits always refer to the environment supplied to the invocation, including after an ancestral change of variables.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.1", "orig": "Lemma 6.1, “A uniform affine spectral gap.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For fixed \\(H\\ge3\\), let \\(G_H=\\mathrm{SL}_H(\\mathbb Z)\\ltimes\\mathbb Z^H\\).", "statement": "<p>For fixed \\(H\\ge3\\), let \\(G_H=\\mathrm{SL}_H(\\mathbb Z)\\ltimes\\mathbb Z^H\\). A fixed finite symmetric generating multiset admits a lazy rational random walk whose action on every finite transitive \\(G_H\\)-set has spectral gap bounded below by a positive constant depending only on \\(H\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Take elementary matrices with one off-diagonal entry \\(\\pm1\\), together with translations \\(\\pm e_i\\), as \\(S\\). In a unitary representation put\n\\[\n\\delta=\\max_{g\\in S}\\|\\rho(g)\\xi-\\xi\\|.\n\\]\nFor each coordinate pair \\(i,j\\), let \\(P_{ij}\\) project onto vectors fixed by that pair's translations. Relative property \\((T)\\), with constant \\(1/10\\), gives\n\\[\n\\|\\xi-P_{ij}\\xi\\|\\le10\\delta.\n\\]\nThese projections commute because they are limits of averages of commuting translations. Their product \\(P_{\\mathrm{tr}}\\) projects onto all translation-invariant vectors. With \\(q=\\binom H2\\),\n\\[\n\\|\\xi-P_{\\mathrm{tr}}\\xi\\|\\le10q\\delta.\n\\]\nNormality makes \\(P_{\\mathrm{tr}}\\) commute with the representation. On its range, the representation factors through \\(\\mathrm{SL}_H(\\mathbb Z)\\). Its Kazhdan constant \\(\\kappa_H\\) gives\n\\[\n\\|\\xi-P_{\\mathrm{inv}}\\xi\\|\n\\le(10q+\\kappa_H^{-1})\\delta=C_H\\delta.\n\\]</p><p>For a finite transitive permutation action, invariant functions are constants. The operator\n\\[\nK_H=\\tfrac12I+\\frac1{2|S|}\\sum_{g\\in S}\\rho(g)\n\\]\nis self-adjoint with spectrum in \\([0,1]\\). For \\(f\\perp1\\),\n\\[\n\\langle f,(I-K_H)f\\rangle\n=\\frac1{4|S|}\\sum_{g\\in S}\\|\\rho(g)f-f\\|_2^2\n\\ge\\frac1{4|S|C_H^2}\\|f\\|_2^2.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.2", "orig": "Lemma 6.2, “Conditional averaging.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Under Proposition 4's conditional law, there is a rational Markov operator \\(K\\) with stationary distribution that law and \\( \\|K^d-\\Pi\\|_{L^s\\to L^s}\\le C_s2^{-c_sd}. \\) The constants are independent of field size …", "statement": "<p>Under Proposition 4's conditional law, there is a rational Markov operator \\(K\\) with stationary distribution that law and\n\\[\n\\|K^d-\\Pi\\|_{L^s\\to L^s}\\le C_s2^{-c_sd}.\n\\]\nThe constants are independent of field size, retention side lengths, free-bit length, identifiers, and frozen bits.</p>", "proof": "<p>On a channel conditioned at \\(y\\) with side \\(L\\), use coordinates\n\\[\n(F,w),\\qquad w=Av(y)\\in\\{0,\\ldots,L-1\\}^H.\n\\]\nThe frame \\(F\\) is uniform and independent of uniform \\(w\\); the constant column is uniquely determined. Identify the box with \\((\\mathbb Z/L\\mathbb Z)^H\\), and act by\n\\[\n(F,w)\\mapsto(MF,Mw+c),\n\\]\nreducing the first coordinate modulo \\(P\\) and the second modulo \\(L\\).</p><p>This action is transitive. Elementary reductions generate \\(\\mathrm{SL}_H(\\mathbb F_P)\\), which is transitive on ordered frames of length \\(r&lt;H\\): extend frames to bases and adjust a complementary column to obtain determinant one. A translation then sets \\(w\\). For an unconditioned channel use a public point with side \\(P\\).</p><p>If the free-bit length is \\(Hq\\), identify it with \\((\\mathbb Z/2^q\\mathbb Z)^H\\) and use its affine action. Concatenate all free blocks into this one component. A bounded number of components remains, so choosing a component uniformly and applying Lemma 4.1 preserves a uniform positive gap.</p><p>Thus \\(\\|K^d-\\Pi\\|_{2\\to2}\\le(1-\\gamma)^d\\), while \\(\\|K^d-\\Pi\\|_{\\infty\\to\\infty}\\le2\\). Riesz–Thorin interpolation gives\n\\[\n\\|K^d-\\Pi\\|_{s\\to s}\n\\le2^{1-2/s}(1-\\gamma)^{2d/s}.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.3", "orig": "Lemma 6.3, “Validated walk variants.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "A length-\\(d\\) word of Lemma 4.2 expands into a bounded-alphabet family of variants described by \\(O(d)\\) bits such that:", "statement": "<p>A length-\\(d\\) word of Lemma 4.2 expands into a bounded-alphabet family of variants described by \\(O(d)\\) bits such that:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. Every fixed variant is a measure-preserving bijection of the full environment before tests.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. Its transformation is independent of the indicated identifiers.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. For each word and environment in its conditional slice, exactly one variant passes all slice and wrap tests, giving the conditional walk transformation.</p><p class=\"li num\">4. Summing valid variants with their original word probabilities equals the conditional average.</p><p>When field elements, identifiers, and the auxiliary bit string have \\(O(B)\\) bits and \\(d=O(B)\\), transformations and tests use \\(O(B)\\) shared scratch.</p>", "proof": "<p>For a fixed affine generator \\((M,c)\\) on a side-\\(L\\) channel, enumerate\n\\[\nA'=MA+(c+Lz)e_1^{\\mathsf T}\\pmod P,\n\\]\nwhere \\(z\\) lies in a fixed finite integer box containing every possible wrap. Fixed generators bound \\(Mw+c\\) by a constant multiple of \\(L\\), so one box works for all \\(L\\ge1\\).</p><p>Each transformation is invertible, preserves the frame restriction and full-channel uniform measure, and does not refer to the retained identifier. At that identifier, check that \\(w=Av(y)\\) is in the box and that \\(Mw+c+Lz\\) lies in it using integer arithmetic. The unique valid wrap is\n\\[\nz_i=-\\lfloor(Mw+c)_i/L\\rfloor.\n\\]\nRepeat at each step. Unconditioned channels may use ordinary field reduction. Free-bit affine operations are invertible; frozen bits stay fixed.</p><p>There are constantly many choices per step, hence \\(O(d)\\)-bit descriptions. Unique wrap validation retains one variant with the original word's weight, not that weight divided by the number of guesses. Polynomial evaluations and fixed-dimensional arithmetic use \\(O(B)\\) scratch. Tests may be delayed until the relevant vertex is current and matrix states regenerated from recorded words.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The additive difference schedule below yields, at every dyadic slack-prefix \\(d_j\\), \\( \\|\\text{mean discrepancy}\\|\\le c_*2^{-p+B_1d_j}, \\qquad \\|\\text{prefix noise}\\|\\le c_*2^{-p-K_nd_j}. \\) Here \\(B_1\\) is fixed …", "statement": "<p>Additive schedule and conditional prefix estimates.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the exact hierarchy, atom interfaces, and conditional walks of Propositions 2–4, with \\(s=8\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Order tasks by stage: transition bins; at each pilot step its score, detector vector, and correction; actual corrections; routed residual bins; reward update. Put each preparation immediately before its normalized task.</p><p class=\"li\">Assign integer ranks \\(i_*\\) decreasing by at least one along every dependency, with \\(i_*=O(l+1)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A task of budget \\(M\\ge0\\), scale \\(a\\), and start rate \\(u\\) has precision\n\\[\np=\\frac M{K_{\\mathrm{prec}}}\n-\\frac12\\log(1/a)-\\frac12\\log(1/u)-A_*i_*.\n\\]\nFor vectors use \\(a=f=b_l\\); detector vectors also use \\(u=f\\). If \\(p\\le0\\), return zero and empty access classes.</p><p class=\"li\">Earlier tasks have error at most \\(C_b2^{-p'}\\) in the prescribed root row metrics and ordinary vector metrics.</p><p class=\"li\">First-channel mean discrepancies are measured by\n\\[\n\\min\\!\\left(1,\\frac{|\\widehat d-d|}{1+d}\\right);\n\\]\nsecond-channel and vector discrepancies use ordinary absolute norms.</p><p class=\"li\">For the deterministic-mean and noise conclusion, local endpoint grouping is exact, and each conditional walk varies every coordinate on which its sample function depends.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The additive difference schedule below yields, at every dyadic slack-prefix \\(d_j\\),\n\\[\n\\|\\text{mean discrepancy}\\|\\le c_*2^{-p+B_1d_j},\n\\qquad\n\\|\\text{prefix noise}\\|\\le c_*2^{-p-K_nd_j}.\n\\]\nHere \\(B_1\\) is fixed, \\(K_n\\) can be prescribed arbitrarily large, and \\(c_*\\) can be made arbitrarily small by task-separation and walk constants. Immediate candidates at slack \\(d\\) have \\(O(d)\\)-bit descriptions and children of budget at most \\(M-d\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 5.1 identifies the targets. Lemmas 5.2–5.4 determine the scale and source-domain costs and omitted-bin error. Lemma 5.5 defines the additive schedule and its path contract. Lemmas 5.6–5.8 give the mean/noise prefix bounds. Exact base terms have no noise. These prefixes remain intermediate arrays; row functions are applied only after completion and compression.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "Lemma 7.1, “True sample means.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For a row pair use the slice \\(\\mathcal C_{xy}^{u,a}=\\{X_u(x)=Y_a(y)=1\\}\\).", "statement": "<p>For a row pair use the slice \\(\\mathcal C_{xy}^{u,a}=\\{X_u(x)=Y_a(y)=1\\}\\). Put \\(\\tau=\\min(a,b)\\). Before division by the current first-channel scale \\(a\\), use\n\\[\nT_b(x,y)=b\\frac{\\pi_a}{\\pi_\\tau}\\mathcal B_b(x,y),\n\\]\n\\[\nT_b^{\\mathrm{prod}}(x,y)=\nb\\frac{\\pi_a}{\\pi_\\tau}\\frac f{\\pi_f}\n\\sum_{z:Z_f(z)=1}\\mathcal F(x,z)\\mathcal B_b(z,y).\n\\]\nThe left factor has roles \\(X,Z\\), rates \\((u;f,f)\\); the right has roles \\(Z,Y\\), rates \\((f;b,\\tau)\\). The negative correction term uses \\(-T_f\\) with \\(\\mathcal F\\).</p><p>A projected term from \\(x_i,y_j\\) to parents is\n\\[\nr_i(x)b\\frac{\\pi_a}{\\pi_\\tau}\\mathcal B_b^{(j)}(x,y),\n\\]\npartitioned by fixed copy symbols and using the corresponding padded channel arguments.</p><p>The second channel is \\(\\zeta\\) times the preparatory conditional mean on \\(\\mathcal C_{xy}^{u,t}\\), extended by zero off \\(Y_t(y)\\). Detector vectors average\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}_J\\{\\mathcal S_i(x,y):X_f(x)=1\\}\n\\]\non \\(Y_f(y)=1\\). Reward terms are\n\\[\nr_i(x)W_l(x),\\qquad\nr_i(x)\\frac f{\\pi_f}\\sum_{z:Z_f(z)=1}\\mathcal F(x,z)W_l(z).\n\\]</p><p>With true atoms these means equal the prescribed hierarchy inputs, preparatory means, detector vectors, and reward updates.</p>", "proof": "<p>If \\(b\\ge a\\), the usage hit is the conditioning hit, and bin normalization gives the deterministic contribution. If \\(b&lt;a\\), nested boxes give\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\!\\left[\\frac{\\pi_a}{\\pi_b}1_{\\{Y_b(y)=1\\}}V\n\\mid\\mathcal C_{xy}^{u,a}\\right]\n=\\mathbb E[V\\mid\\mathcal C_{xy}^{u,b}].\n\\]</p><p>For a product, first change terminal conditioning if needed, then condition on \\(Z_f(z)=1\\). Apart from membership, the true right bin depends only on \\(Y\\), while the true left correction depends on \\(X,Z\\). Their normalizations therefore apply separately. The shared midpoint role does not remain in the right bin's random value after its hit is fixed. The factor \\(f/\\pi_f\\) cancels midpoint sampling and converts the correction atom to its entry. Sum over midpoints.</p><p>Projections multiply by deterministic \\(r_i\\), and routed fractions are already in the bin; fixed-suffix invariance applies on each copy part. The second-channel mean and detector mean are their definitions. True rewards are deterministic, so the same midpoint argument proves the reward identity.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.2", "orig": "Lemma 7.2, “Homogeneous loss.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For \\(H,H'\\ge0\\), define \\( \\mathcal L(H,H')= \\begin{cases} |H-H'|^s/(H+H')^{s-1},&H+H'>0,\\\\ 0,&H=H'=0. \\end{cases} \\) It is comparable, with constants depending only on \\(s\\), to \\( |H^{1/s}-(H')^{1/s}|^s, \\) and …", "statement": "<p>For \\(H,H'\\ge0\\), define\n\\[\n\\mathcal L(H,H')=\n\\begin{cases}\n|H-H'|^s/(H+H')^{s-1},&amp;H+H'&gt;0,\\\\\n0,&amp;H=H'=0.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nIt is comparable, with constants depending only on \\(s\\), to\n\\[\n|H^{1/s}-(H')^{1/s}|^s,\n\\]\nand contracts under sums and nonnegative mixtures.</p><p>If \\(H\\le C_0G\\), \\(G\\ge0\\), then for every \\(a&gt;0,H'\\ge0\\),\n\\[\na\\min\\!\\left(1,\\frac{|H-H'|}{a+G}\\right)^s\n\\le C\\mathcal L(H,H').\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(v=\\max(H^{1/s},(H')^{1/s})\\), \\(w=\\min(H^{1/s},(H')^{1/s})\\). Factor \\(v^s-w^s\\) between \\(v^{s-1}(v-w)\\) and \\(sv^{s-1}(v-w)\\); the denominator lies between \\(v^{s(s-1)}\\) and \\(2^{s-1}v^{s(s-1)}\\).</p><p>For mixtures apply Hölder to\n\\[\n|H-H'|=\\mathcal L(H,H')^{1/s}(H+H')^{(s-1)/s},\n\\]\nusing the triangle inequality before integration.</p><p>If \\(H'\\le2H+a\\), then \\(H+H'\\le3C_0G+a\\le C'(a+G)\\), giving the last bound directly. Otherwise \\(|H-H'|&gt;H'/2\\), \\(H+H'&lt;3H'/2\\), and \\(H'&gt;a\\), so \\(\\mathcal L(H,H')\\ge ca\\), while the left side is at most \\(a\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.3", "orig": "Lemma 7.3, “Scale and domain comparison.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Compare a budget-\\(m\\) sample term from Lemma 5.1 with its true-atom version.", "statement": "<p>Compare a budget-\\(m\\) sample term from Lemma 5.1 with its true-atom version. The scale factor in the \\(s\\)-th-power mean-error bound multiplying a child root-discrepancy power is, up to constants,\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nb/a,&amp;b\\ge a,\\\\\n(b/a)^{s-1},&amp;b&lt;a.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nA child that releases the old source and restarts on a stage-rate \\(f\\) domain costs additionally at most \\(f/u\\) in norm power. Direct children retaining the source have no such factor.</p><p>Same-scale vectors and second channels have constant scale cost, with \\(f/u\\) only for source release. No independence of estimated factors is assumed.</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(b\\ge a\\), apply Lemma 5.2 after conditional expectation, and mixture contraction before it. Homogeneity supplies \\(b/a\\). The factor \\(f/\\pi_f\\) is bounded.</p><p>Split bounded product-root differences into factor-root differences with support indicators of unaffected factors. For a left discrepancy, the right row cap eliminates endpoints. For a right discrepancy, the left incoming cap eliminates old starts. Explicitly, if \\(P\\) has incoming degree at most \\(K_c\\) and the right discrepancy \\(e(z,y)\\) has one common tuple,\n\\[\n\\frac1N\\mathbb E\\sum_x\\frac{X_u(x)}u\n\\sum_{z:(x,z)\\in P}Z_f(z)\\sum_{y:Y_b(y)=1}|e(z,y)|^s\n\\le K_c\\frac fu\\|e\\|_{f,b;s}^s.\n\\]\nThis is pointwise in the shared environment. A projected source-multiplier discrepancy similarly costs \\(f_{\\mathrm{par}}/u\\). Partition by fixed copy symbols to retain common tuples.</p><p>For \\(b&lt;a\\), change conditioning to \\(Y_b\\) before taking powers. Bounded row path counts give ordinary finite-sum comparisons, and\n\\[\n(b/a)^s\\frac{\\pi_a}{\\pi_b}=O((b/a)^{s-1}).\n\\]\nBounded ordinary differences are controlled by root differences.</p><p>For the second channel, Jensen is applied on \\(\\mathcal C_{xy}^{u,t}\\); the \\(Y_t\\) mask is already inside the preparatory broad domain, so no \\(\\pi_a/\\pi_t\\) penalty occurs. Detector rank differences use supremum bounded by sum. Rewards use bounded left paths and the same incoming-cap source elimination. Unsigned terms of the signed residual are dominated by the residual by Proposition 2, so their clipped relative errors combine by triangle inequalities.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.4", "orig": "Lemma 7.4, “Fine omission.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Omitting one fine bin of scale \\(b<a\\) gives normalized contribution norm at most \\( C(b/a)^{(s-1)/s}. \\) This holds for direct bins, routed bins, and correction–bin products.", "statement": "<p>Omitting one fine bin of scale \\(b&lt;a\\) gives normalized contribution norm at most\n\\[\nC(b/a)^{(s-1)/s}.\n\\]\nThis holds for direct bins, routed bins, and correction–bin products.</p>", "proof": "<p>The deterministic contribution \\(H\\) has entries at most \\(Cb\\) and bounded row mass, including products by Lemma 3.2. Thus\n\\[\n\\sum_y\\pi_a|H(x,y)/a|^s\n\\le\\frac{\\pi_a}{a^s}(Cb)^{s-1}\\sum_yH(x,y)\n\\le C'(b/a)^{s-1}.\n\\]\nIntegrating starts gives \\(\\pi_u/u=O(1)\\), and summing at most \\(N\\) rows cancels \\(1/N\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.5", "orig": "Lemma 7.5, “Immediate paths.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For a first-channel scale \\(b\\), let \\(h_b=\\log(b/a)\\) and \\( c_b=\\lceil c_0(1+|h_b|)\\rceil. \\) At parent budget \\(M\\), replace all earlier factors in a sample by budget-\\(m\\) estimates and form …", "statement": "<p>For a first-channel scale \\(b\\), let \\(h_b=\\log(b/a)\\) and\n\\[\nc_b=\\lceil c_0(1+|h_b|)\\rceil.\n\\]\nAt parent budget \\(M\\), replace all earlier factors in a sample by budget-\\(m\\) estimates and form\n\\[\nT_{b,m}-T_{b,m-1}\\qquad(1\\le m\\le M-c_b),\n\\]\nwhere first-channel terms include division by \\(a\\). Budget-zero sample terms are zero; exact base terms \\(S/a,W_0\\) are inserted separately. Use zero scale offset for preparatory means, detector trials, and fixed-stage reward terms. Final correction multipliers have a separate fixed positive budget drop \\(c_{\\mathrm{col}}\\).</p><p>Apply the same conditional walk of length \\(\\ell(M-m)\\), with the same guessed wraps, to both parts. Group layers by dyadic slack \\(d\\le M-m&lt;2d\\), from larger to smaller \\(d\\), including empty groups.</p><p>Immediate candidates at slack \\(d\\) have \\(O(d)\\)-bit descriptions. Exact endpoint sums equal the conditional-walk increments. Products reach a midpoint by a paired correction move before querying the right factor; its tuple contains no discarded old source. Every child has budget at most \\(M-d\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>A descriptor records finitely many formula and copy choices, scale and budget offsets, sign, generator and wrap word, and bounded constituent ports. Included scales satisfy \\(|h_b|=O(d)\\), and the walk length is \\(O(d)\\). There are \\(O((1+d)^2)\\) budget and scale choices before the exponential word choices, so the total description is \\(O(d)\\).</p><p>Validate constituent ports, memberships, and wraps at the vertices where their coordinates are available. For a fixed endpoint and original word, unique validation leaves its genuine conditional transformation with that word's original probability. Distinct-entry ports enumerate pairs once. True-ID endpoint grouping therefore gives the walk of the paired difference.</p><p>A correction's bounded paired move supplies the midpoint and inverse token. The right \\(Z,Y\\) tuple has no old source. A direct unbalanced terminal motion is followed by no factor query at its endpoint. The budget bound follows from the slack definition.</p><p>Along a dependency chain, scale extensions cost \\(O(d)\\) reciprocal-rate logarithms while budget drops by at least \\(d\\). Stage resets and suffix lengths are bounded by the hierarchy. Hence all reachable rate logarithms are \\(O(B)\\) for \\(M_{\\mathrm{top}}=O(B)\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.6", "orig": "Lemma 7.6, “Mean prefixes.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Under Proposition 5's earlier-task bounds, every active mean prefix through slack \\(d_j\\) has discrepancy at most \\( c_*2^{-p+B_1d_j}. \\) For the first channel use clipped relative discrepancy; for the second and …", "statement": "<p>Under Proposition 5's earlier-task bounds, every active mean prefix through slack \\(d_j\\) has discrepancy at most\n\\[\nc_*2^{-p+B_1d_j}.\n\\]\nFor the first channel use clipped relative discrepancy; for the second and vectors use ordinary discrepancy. \\(B_1\\) is fixed independently of the later choice of \\(A_*\\); increasing \\(A_*\\) makes \\(c_*\\) arbitrarily small.</p>", "proof": "<p>For a child at \\((m,a',u',i_*')\\), subtract precision definitions:\n\\[\np'-p=-\\frac{M-m}{K_{\\mathrm{prec}}}\n+\\frac12\\log(a'/a)+\\frac12\\log(u'/u)\n+A_*(i_*-i_*').\n\\]\nCombining Lemma 5.3 with \\(a'\\ge cb\\) gives, in norm,\n\\[\nCC_b2^{-p-A_*+(M-m)/K_{\\mathrm{prec}}}2^{-3|h_b|/8}.\n\\]\nFor source release, precision gains \\(\\tfrac12\\log(f/u)\\), leaving\n\\[\n(f/u)^{1/8-1/2}\\le1.\n\\]\nThe separate column call similarly has its fixed budget loss paid by rank separation.</p><p>The mean prefix telescopes to budget\n\\[\nm_j(b)=\\max(0,M-\\max(d_j,c_b)).\n\\]\nThus \\(M-m_j(b)\\le d_j+c_0(1+|h_b|)+1\\). Choose \\(c_0/K_{\\mathrm{prec}}&lt;1/8\\). Summing the resulting\n\\[\nC'C_b2^{-p-A_*+d_j/K_{\\mathrm{prec}}}2^{-|h_b|/4}\n\\]\nover the geometric rate grid costs a constant, including inactive children.</p><p>Wholly omitted scales have \\(c_b\\ge M\\). Activity and \\(K_{\\mathrm{prec}}/2&gt;c_0\\) exclude coarse omitted bins; the sparse direct correction is also available after fixed rank separation. For omitted fine bins,\n\\[\n|h_b|\\ge(M-1)/c_0-1.\n\\]\nLemma 5.4 and a geometric sum give \\(C2^{-(s-1)M/(sc_0)}\\). Choose \\(K_{\\mathrm{prec}}&gt;sc_0/(s-1)\\); since \\(p\\le M/K_{\\mathrm{prec}}\\), this is \\(C2^{-p-\\eta M}\\). Activity forces \\(M&gt;K_{\\mathrm{prec}}A_*i_*\\), so its coefficient is arbitrarily small. Any fixed \\(B_1&gt;1/K_{\\mathrm{prec}}\\) suffices.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.7", "orig": "Lemma 7.7, “Absolute differences.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "At slack \\(d\\), the ordinary raw norm of an unaveraged paired difference is at most \\( CC_b2^{-p-A_*+C_0d}, \\) with \\(C_0\\) independent of the walk-length coefficient.", "statement": "<p>At slack \\(d\\), the ordinary raw norm of an unaveraged paired difference is at most\n\\[\nCC_b2^{-p-A_*+C_0d},\n\\]\nwith \\(C_0\\) independent of the walk-length coefficient.</p><p>For two coupled versions whose budget-\\(r\\) child discrepancies are bounded by \\(2^{-p_r}\\lambda_r\\), a budget-\\(r\\) sample discrepancy is bounded by\n\\[\n2^{-p+C_0'd}\\lambda_r\n\\]\nsummed over its fixed dependency types. A paired layer uses \\(\\lambda_m+\\lambda_{m-1}\\), and every child has budget at most \\(M-d\\).</p><p>These bounds hold after each fixed measure-preserving variant and its valid endpoint restriction. Counting variants separately enlarges \\(C_0'\\) by a walk-dependent constant.</p>", "proof": "<p>Subtract the same stochastic true-atom term from both consecutive-budget samples. Product comparisons use bounded factors, bounded left path counts, row caps, and incoming caps exactly as in Lemma 5.3, but without averaging. Coarse absolute scale cost is \\(b/a\\), and fine importance factors may remain inside powers; both are \\(2^{O(|h_b|)}\\). The precision identity pays source reset \\(f/u\\). All remaining changes are \\(2^{O(d)}\\).</p><p>Replace comparison to truth by comparison of coupled children for the second assertion. For a fixed variant, change variables by its full-environment bijection and discard surplus validity guards, retaining the child-domain sums. Equal actual endpoints must be combined before measuring the paired difference. The \\(2^{O(d)}\\) variant count only enlarges the crude exponent.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.8", "orig": "Lemma 7.8, “Conditional prefix bounds.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Under Proposition 5's exact-grouping and full-averaging hypotheses, let \\(S_j^{\\mathrm{mean}}\\) be the uniform conditional-mean prefix and \\(S_j^{\\mathrm{noise}}=S_j-S_j^{\\mathrm{mean}}\\).", "statement": "<p>Under Proposition 5's exact-grouping and full-averaging hypotheses, let \\(S_j^{\\mathrm{mean}}\\) be the uniform conditional-mean prefix and \\(S_j^{\\mathrm{noise}}=S_j-S_j^{\\mathrm{mean}}\\). Then\n\\[\n\\left\\|\\min\\!\\left(1,\n\\frac{|S_j^{1,\\mathrm{mean}}-d|}{1+d}\\right)\\right\\|_{u,a;s}\n\\le c_*2^{-p+B_1d_j},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\|S_j^{\\mathrm{noise}}\\|_{u,a;s}\n\\le c_*2^{-p-K_nd_j}.\n\\]\nSecond-channel means use ordinary discrepancy with their usage masks; vectors have the corresponding vertex bounds. Slice means are deterministic at each fixed pair or vertex.</p>", "proof": "<p>The mean bound is Lemma 5.6. For a paired endpoint function \\(F_{xy}\\), let \\(A_{xy}\\) be its walk operator, \\(\\Pi_{xy}\\) uniform slice averaging, and\n\\[\nw_{xy}=\\Pr(\\mathcal C_{xy}^{u,a})/(Nu).\n\\]\nThe uniform operator estimate gives\n\\[\n\\sum_{x,y}w_{xy}\\|(A_{xy}-\\Pi_{xy})F_{xy}\\|_{L^s}^s\n\\le r_{M-m}^s\\sum_{x,y}w_{xy}\\|F_{xy}\\|_{L^s}^s,\n\\]\nwhere \\(r_{M-m}=C2^{-c\\ell(M-m)}\\). The right sum is exactly the integrated raw norm bounded by Lemma 5.7.</p><p>For second channels use \\(Y_t\\) in both slice and weight; vectors use one-vertex slices. There is no additional conditioning penalty. Choose \\(\\ell\\) large enough to dominate \\(2^{C_0d}\\) and the polynomial group count, leaving \\(2^{-(K_n+1)d}\\). Sum larger-slack groups.</p><p>Contraction is applied to the complete same-seed paired difference after valid variants have been summed into the walk. Uniform averaging integrates every coordinate used by that function, hence leaves a deterministic pairwise mean, apart from the prescribed second-channel membership mask.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Fixed compression and range-clamping constants can be chosen so that exact-grouped active row outputs, scores, and witnesses, followed by incoming and multiplier assembly, have error at most \\( (C_b/2)2^{-p}. \\) Final …", "statement": "<p>Compression reserve with exact endpoint grouping.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix a row task with rates \\(u,a,t\\), true first signal \\(d_y\\ge0\\), and optional second target\n\\[\nv_y=\\zeta1_{\\{Y_t(y)=1\\}}q_y,\n\\]\nwhere \\(d_y,q_y\\) are deterministic at each pair, \\(0\\le q_y\\le1\\), and \\(q_y=0\\) below the preparation's positive support threshold.</p><p class=\"li\">Assume\n\\[\na\\sum_yd_y\\le C_{\\mathrm{mass}}.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal H\\) be the finite family of pre-incoming functions, auxiliary scores, and witnesses from Proposition 3. Their \\(s\\)-th roots are bounded and Lipschitz on fixed sensitivity ranges. Every member has entry threshold \\(e_H\\), gate threshold \\(g_H\\), and \\(3g_H&lt;e_H\\). Gates are full near rank zero.</p><p class=\"li\">Include arbitrary dummy coordinates with target and increments zero. A compressed row has common default \\((z,0)\\), also used by dummies.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(d_1&gt;\\cdots&gt;d_{m_*}=1\\) be decreasing powers of two, \\(S_i\\) the exact-grouped raw prefixes, and \\(\\Delta_i=S_i-S_{i-1}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n\\[\ne_i(y)=\\max\\left\\{\n\\min\\!\\left(1,\\frac{|S_i^1(y)-d_y|}{1+d_y}\\right),\n\\min(1,|S_i^2(y)-v_y|)\n\\right\\},\n\\quad e_i=\\sup_ye_i(y).\n\\]\nOmit channel two if absent. Write \\(e_i(y)\\le\\beta_i(y)+\\gamma_i(y)\\), where \\(\\beta_i\\) is mean discrepancy and \\(\\gamma_i\\) absolute noise.</p><p class=\"li\">For row integration set\n\\[\n\\int F\\,d\\mu=\\frac1N\\mathbb E\\sum_x\\frac{X_u(x)}uF(x).\n\\]\nAssume the endpoint-integrated bounds\n\\[\n\\left(\\int\\sum_y\\beta_i(y)^s\\,d\\mu\\right)^{1/s}\n\\le c_*2^{-p+B_1d_i},\n\\quad\n\\left(\\int\\sum_y\\gamma_i(y)^s\\,d\\mu\\right)^{1/s}\n\\le c_*2^{-p-K_nd_i}.\n\\]\nMean-error magnitudes are deterministic at fixed pairs, with the narrower second-channel mask.</p><p class=\"li\">Earlier task errors and common-parameter support contracts hold as in Proposition 5.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Fixed compression and range-clamping constants can be chosen so that exact-grouped active row outputs, scores, and witnesses, followed by incoming and multiplier assembly, have error at most\n\\[\n(C_b/2)2^{-p}.\n\\]\nFinal clipped vectors have the same reserve. This is original Proposition 8.7, “Error reserve with exact endpoint grouping.”</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 5 supplies the deterministic-mean and absolute-noise hypotheses. Proposition 3 supplies the threshold separations, bounded supports, and root regularity. Lemma 6.5 gives error \\(C_{\\mathrm{buf}}c_*2^{-p}\\) for all compressed pre-incoming functions. Lemma 6.6 preserves those function values under exact-ID common increments.</p><p>Proposition 3's assembly comparison charges incoming gates to the same pre-output errors. Separate column-vector calls have a fixed budget drop and lower task rank; the precision identity pays \\(f/u\\), and increasing \\(A_*\\) reduces the remaining coefficient. Vectors require no row compression and use final slack \\(1\\) and nonexpansive clipping.</p><p>Choose the bounded-output constant first; then cutoff and precision constants, prefix mean exponent, compression floors and capacities, and required noise decay. Choose task separation and walk length to make \\(c_*\\) small enough that the total is at most \\(C_b/2\\). Inactive tasks keep their separate trivial \\(C_b\\) bound. The other half is reserved for fingerprint error.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.1", "orig": "Lemma 8.1, “Local compression properties.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "At group \\(j\\), add \\(\\Delta_j\\) by actual endpoint equality to obtain \\(w_y\\), and define \\( \\rho_j=2^{-c_1d_j},\\quad n_j=2^{c_2d_j},\\quad m_y=\\max_\\nu|w_y^\\nu|, \\) …", "statement": "<p>At group \\(j\\), add \\(\\Delta_j\\) by actual endpoint equality to obtain \\(w_y\\), and define\n\\[\n\\rho_j=2^{-c_1d_j},\\quad n_j=2^{c_2d_j},\\quad\nm_y=\\max_\\nu|w_y^\\nu|,\n\\]\n\\[\nu_0=\\max(z,\\rho_j,\\operatorname{rank}_{n_j}(m)),\n\\quad q_0=\\max(0,\\operatorname{rank}_K(w^1)),\n\\]\n\\[\nz'=\\max(u_0,\\min(q_0,3u_0)).\n\\]\nAssume \\(n_j\\ge K\\). With \\(b=(z',0)\\), update\n\\[\nT_j^\\nu(y)=b^\\nu+\n\\operatorname{clip}\\bigl(w_y^\\nu-b^\\nu,\\,\n\\pm7(m_y-2u_0)_+\\bigr).\n\\]</p><p>The update replaces size-\\(\\le2u_0\\) positions by default, is identity at size-\\(\\ge3u_0\\) positions, and lies coordinatewise between input and new default. The default is nondecreasing. The full-array/default map is uniformly sup-norm Lipschitz. At most \\(n_j\\) positions, chosen from the old list and new candidates, suffice to represent all exceptions.</p>", "proof": "<p>The zero radius proves replacement. Since \\(z'\\le3u_0\\), at \\(m_y\\ge3u_0\\),\n\\[\n|w_y^1-z'|\\le m_y+3u_0\\le7(m_y-2u_0),\n\\qquad |w_y^2|\\le7(m_y-2u_0),\n\\]\nso the update is identity. Clipping a displacement toward zero gives the coordinatewise assertion, and \\(z'\\ge u_0\\ge z\\).</p><p>A \\(\\delta\\) perturbation changes sizes, ranks, \\(u_0,q_0\\) by at most \\(\\delta\\), new defaults by at most \\(3\\delta\\), and radii by at most \\(21\\delta\\). Since\n\\[\n|\\operatorname{clip}(r,\\pm A)-\\operatorname{clip}(r',\\pm A')|\n\\le|r-r'|+|A-A'|,\n\\]\na fixed constant, for example \\(28\\), bounds the update's Lipschitz factor.</p><p>Fewer than \\(n_j\\) positions have size strictly above \\(u_0\\). A buffered test accepting every size \\(&gt;2u_0\\) and only sizes \\(&gt;u_0\\) contains all possible exceptions within the cap. Outside old and new candidates the input is \\((z,0)\\), hence becomes default. Extra listed real default-valued positions do not change the array; dummies are never selected.</p><p>A perturbation introduced at group \\(i\\) propagates with factor\n\\[\nCC_1^{m_*-i+1}\\le C(d_i+1)^{\\log_2C_1}.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.2", "orig": "Lemma 8.2, “Preloss and stationary reference.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Call group \\(j\\) an overflow if \\(u_0>\\rho_j\\).", "statement": "<p>Call group \\(j\\) an overflow if \\(u_0&gt;\\rho_j\\). Choose \\(c_1\\) large enough that\n\\[\n6\\sum_{i&lt;j}\\rho_i\\le\\rho_j/100,\\qquad\n6\\sum_i\\rho_i\\le\\eta/100,\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\eta&gt;0\\) lies below all entry and rank sensitivity thresholds, and \\(\\zeta\\le\\eta/100\\).</p><p>Before the first overflow \\(j\\), put \\(L_{j-1}=T_{j-1}-S_{j-1}\\). Then\n\\[\n\\|L_{j-1}\\|_\\infty\\le6\\sum_{i&lt;j}\\rho_i,\n\\]\nand at \\(d_y\\ge\\eta\\),\n\\[\n\\max_\\nu|L_{j-1}^\\nu(y)|\n\\le C\\sum_{i&lt;j}\\rho_ie_i(y).\n\\]</p><p>Start a stationary reference at group \\(j\\) with \\((d,v)+L_{j-1}\\), the same prior default, and no further increments. Every subsequent compression preserves its relevant row functions. Their root discrepancy from truth is at most \\(C\\sum_{i&lt;j}\\rho_ie_i\\).</p><p>If no overflow occurs, the final discrepancy is at most\n\\[\nC\\left(e_{m_*}+\\sum_i\\rho_ie_i\\right)\n\\]\nin row supremum.</p>", "proof": "<p>Before overflow, each compression changes a coordinate by at most \\(6\\rho_i\\), only at size below \\(3\\rho_i\\). This gives the uniform bound. If such a change occurs where \\(d_y\\ge\\eta\\), the raw first-channel relative error is bounded below by a positive fixed \\(c_\\eta\\), because prior preloss is much smaller than \\(\\rho_i\\). The change is therefore at most \\(C\\rho_ie_i(y)\\).</p><p>In the reference, negative first magnitudes and all second magnitudes are at most \\(\\eta\\). Let \\(q\\) be its \\(K\\)-th first rank. It is nonnegative because the default is. If \\(q&gt;\\eta\\), the \\(n_i\\)-th size rank, floor, and prior default are at most \\(q\\), so \\(u_0,z'\\le q\\). Entries at most \\(q\\) stay at most \\(q\\); entries at least \\(q\\) either stay unchanged or are moved toward default \\(q\\). The \\(K\\)-th rank remains \\(q\\). If \\(q\\le\\eta\\), all these ranks and defaults stay at most \\(\\eta\\).</p><p>For a row function whose gate is not zero, every supported entry exceeds \\(e_H&gt;3g_H\\ge3u_0\\) in the relevant case and is unchanged. Smaller entries cannot cross support. In the small-rank case, the gate stays in its full region. Thus reference outputs are preserved.</p><p>Targets below \\(\\eta\\) remain below entry sensitivity and cannot perturb ranks in their sensitivity ranges. At other positions apply the preloss bound, fixed sensitivity clips, and root Lipschitzness. The no-overflow conclusion adds the final raw-prefix error; if its bound is not small, bounded output ranges suffice.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.3", "orig": "Lemma 8.3, “Stability after the first overflow.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "At a first overflow in group \\(j\\), the final row-root discrepancy from Lemma 6.2's stationary reference is at most \\( C\\sum_{i\\ge j}C_1^{m_*-i+1}e_i. \\) The constant is independent of the largest true first entry.", "statement": "<p>At a first overflow in group \\(j\\), the final row-root discrepancy from Lemma 6.2's stationary reference is at most\n\\[\nC\\sum_{i\\ge j}C_1^{m_*-i+1}e_i.\n\\]\nThe constant is independent of the largest true first entry.</p>", "proof": "<p>If the displayed sum is not small, bounded outputs suffice. Otherwise all clipped errors are below one, so first-channel errors and later increments are bounded by \\((1+d_y)e_i\\) and \\((1+d_y)(e_i+e_{i-1})\\); second-channel bounds omit \\(1+d_y\\).</p><p>Choose \\(G\\) above every gate-closing threshold. A baseline at least \\(G\\) permanently closes all gates. Choose \\(L_0\\) much larger than \\(G\\) and all sensitivity thresholds. If at least \\(K\\) true entries exceed \\(L_0\\), reference gates close. In the perturbed run, either some \\(u_0\\ge G\\) closes them permanently, or all these large entries remain above half their true values and above \\(3G\\), hence in the compression identity region. They then close the gates by rank.</p><p>Otherwise fewer than \\(K\\) true entries exceed \\(L_0\\), and reference ranks and thresholds are bounded by \\(2L_0\\). Choose \\(L_1\\) much larger. At the at most \\(K-1\\) positions with \\(d_y&gt;L_1\\), replace first coordinates in both comparisons by common large positive sentinels and set their later first increments to zero. Reference ranks and saturated outputs do not change.</p><p>All other absolute errors are now uniformly bounded by \\(C(1+L_1)e_i\\). Iterating Lemma 6.1 gives the asserted bound and keeps thresholds below \\(2L_0+1\\) when the sum is small.</p><p>Remove the sentinels inductively: original large coordinates stay above \\(d_y/2\\), beyond bounded thresholds and saturation ranges. Since fewer than \\(K\\) such entries occur and \\(n_i\\ge K\\), replacing them cannot change a relevant \\(K\\)-th or \\(n_i\\)-th rank below the threshold bound. Compression is identity there, and all other coordinates agree.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.4", "orig": "Lemma 8.4, “Conditional rarity of a true heavy count.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(O_j\\) be the event that \\(j\\) is the first overflow, and define \\( \\Theta_j=\\{\\#\\{y\\text{ retained}:d_y>\\rho_j/4\\}\\ge n_j/2\\}. \\) At each specified endpoint …", "statement": "<p>Let \\(O_j\\) be the event that \\(j\\) is the first overflow, and define\n\\[\n\\Theta_j=\\{\\#\\{y\\text{ retained}:d_y&gt;\\rho_j/4\\}\\ge n_j/2\\}.\n\\]\nAt each specified endpoint,\n\\[\n\\Pr[\\Theta_j\\mid X_u(x)=Y_a(y)=1]\\le C/(n_j\\rho_j),\n\\]\nalso with \\(Y_t(y)=1\\). Moreover,\n\\[\n\\|1_{\\Theta_j}\\beta_i\\|_{L^s(\\mu)}\n\\le Cc_*2^{-p+B_1d_i}(n_j\\rho_j)^{-1/s}.\n\\]\nOn \\(O_j\\cap\\Theta_j^c\\),\n\\[\n1_{O_j\\cap\\Theta_j^c}\n\\le\\frac C{n_j\\rho_j^s}\\sum_ye_j(y)^s.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The deterministic heavy set has at most \\(4C_{\\mathrm{mass}}/(a\\rho_j)\\) elements. Conditional on one endpoint hit, every other heavy endpoint is retained with probability at most \\(Ca\\), even for a narrower forced hit. Thus the expected count is at most \\(C'/\\rho_j\\), and Markov proves rarity. The independent start condition has no effect.</p><p>At a fixed pair, the mean-error magnitude \\(b_i(y)\\) is deterministic. Bound the retained row supremum power by the sum of \\(Y_a(y)b_i(y)^s\\), then condition on start and endpoint. Rarity multiplies each expected summand by at most \\(C/(n_j\\rho_j)\\). Repeat for the second-channel magnitude with \\(Y_t\\), and use the assumed integrated mean bound. No analogous conditional estimate is asserted for noise.</p><p>At first overflow the previous default is below \\(\\rho_j\\), so at least \\(n_j\\) retained real positions have input size \\(&gt;\\rho_j\\). If \\(\\Theta_j\\) fails, at least half have \\(d_y\\le\\rho_j/4\\), and their second targets vanish. The preloss separation then forces \\(e_j(y)\\ge c\\rho_j\\), proving the indicator bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.5", "orig": "Proposition 8.5, “Integrated compression bound.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Under Proposition 6's hypotheses, choose \\(c_1\\) sufficiently large, then \\(c_2\\) sufficiently large, and \\(K_n\\) sufficiently large.", "statement": "<p>Under Proposition 6's hypotheses, choose \\(c_1\\) sufficiently large, then \\(c_2\\) sufficiently large, and \\(K_n\\) sufficiently large. Compression before range clamping satisfies\n\\[\n\\left(\n\\int\\sum_{H\\in\\mathcal H}\\sum_y\n|H(T_{m_*};y)^{1/s}-H(d,v;y)^{1/s}|^s\\,d\\mu\n\\right)^{1/s}\n\\le C_{\\mathrm{buf}}c_*2^{-p},\n\\]\nwhere \\(C_{\\mathrm{buf}}\\) is independent of group count, row width, and precision.</p>", "proof": "<p>For every raw array the total support over \\(\\mathcal H\\) is bounded; the discrepancy support lies in the union of true and computed supports. Hence row-supremum bounds become output-sum bounds at constant cost.</p><p>Preloss costs at most\n\\[\nCc_*2^{-p}\\sum_i\\rho_i(2^{B_1d_i}+2^{-K_nd_i}).\n\\]\nNo overflow adds the final-prefix bound. False-type first overflows contribute power at most\n\\[\nCc_*^s2^{-sp}\\sum_j\n(n_j\\rho_j^s)^{-1}\n(2^{sB_1d_j}+2^{-sK_nd_j}).\n\\]</p><p>For true-type overflow, Lemma 6.3 and conditional rarity bound the mean contribution by\n\\[\nCc_*2^{-p}\\sum_j(n_j\\rho_j)^{-1/s}\n\\sum_{i\\ge j}C_1^{m_*-i+1}2^{B_1d_i}.\n\\]\nFor noise, the first-overflow events are disjoint, so pointwise\n\\[\n\\sum_j1_{O_j}\\sum_{i\\ge j}C_1^{m_*-i+1}\\gamma_i\n\\le\\sum_iC_1^{m_*-i+1}\\gamma_i.\n\\]\nIts norm is bounded by\n\\[\nCc_*2^{-p}\\sum_iC_1^{m_*-i+1}2^{-K_nd_i}.\n\\]</p><p>Choose \\(c_1&gt;B_1\\) with floor margins, and\n\\[\nc_2&gt;sc_1+sB_1\n\\]\nwith positive slack and \\(n_j\\ge K\\). The false-overflow exponential is \\(2^{-(c_2-sc_1-sB_1)d_j}\\). The true-overflow inner sum is polynomial in \\(d_j+1\\) times \\(2^{B_1d_j}\\); the same stronger choice makes its outer sum converge. Large \\(K_n\\) handles the noise sum. All coefficients are fixed before reducing \\(c_*\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.6", "orig": "Lemma 8.6, “Range clamping.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Suppose common exact-grouped increments satisfy \\(\\|\\Delta_i\\|_\\infty\\le2^{Dd_i}\\).", "statement": "<p>Suppose common exact-grouped increments satisfy \\(\\|\\Delta_i\\|_\\infty\\le2^{Dd_i}\\). Choose a common gate-closing level \\(G\\), then \\(L&gt;10G\\) above all sensitivity thresholds. Let \\(R_i=2^{B_2d_i}\\), with \\(B_2\\) large enough that\n\\[\nR_i\\ge10L+10\\sum_{k&gt;i}\\|\\Delta_k\\|_\\infty.\n\\]\nAfter each compression clip every coordinate and default component to \\([-R_i,R_i]\\).</p><p>These clamps do not change final relevant row functions for the fixed common increments. Each clamp is sup-norm nonexpansive, preserving comparison propagation. The analogous statement holds for vector sums clipped beyond final sensitivity and all remaining additive variation.</p>", "proof": "<p>Compare clamped and unclamped runs. Once both baselines are at least \\(G\\), dummy ranks close all gates permanently; every clamp bound exceeds \\(G\\).</p><p>Before closure, maintain that baselines agree below \\(G\\), while differing components have matching signs, magnitudes beyond \\(L\\), and enough tail margin that future common increments cannot bring them into \\([-L,L]\\). The displayed range bound establishes this at first clipping and refreshes it at later clipping.</p><p>After additions, differing positions have size \\(&gt;L\\). Size ranks either agree below \\(G\\) or both force closure. In the former case \\(u_0\\) agrees; positive differing first entries are above the clipped rank range \\(3u_0\\), and negative ones do not affect its nonnegative part. Thus new baselines agree. Differing positions lie in the compression identity region, while equal positions undergo identical updates.</p><p>All arguments within sensitivity ranges therefore agree; large differing arguments have identical saturated behavior. Scalar clipping is nonexpansive. The vector proof uses the same tail margin without ranks. This equality concerns row functions and witnesses, not finite-accuracy list or port presentations.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every allowed task and its precision \\(p\\), \\( d_{u,a}(\\overline H,H)\\le C_b2^{-p} \\) for all actual row outputs, pre-incoming scores, and support witnesses.", "statement": "<p>Actual estimator error through budget-block fingerprints.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use Propositions 2–6 and their common task tuples, with top statistical budget \\(M_{\\mathrm{top}}=O(B)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Every endpoint has an injective fixed-length binary encoding of length at most \\(c_{\\mathrm{ID}}B\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Choose a fixed small rational \\(\\epsilon&gt;0\\), put\n\\[\nh_*=\\lfloor\\epsilon\\log B\\rfloor,\\quad\nM_b=bh_*,\\quad\nI_b=\\{M_b,\\ldots,M_b+h_*-1\\},\n\\]\nand enlarge the minimum \\(B\\) so \\(h_*\\ge1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Give every budget block an independent \\(O(\\log B)\\)-bit salt, padded to a multiple of \\(H\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A task in block \\(b\\) may inspect only effective salt blocks at most \\(b\\). Its conditional walks vary all earlier salts and freeze its current and later salts. This rule applies to numerical values and every structural access, ordering, validity, and predecessor operation.</p><p class=\"li\">At slack \\(d&gt;2h_*\\), compare IDs exactly. At \\(d\\le2h_*\\), discard invalid candidates individually, sum new increments by the current block's key, add a bucket sum to every old representative with that key, and introduce the first valid new candidate only if no old representative has that key. Old values are never summed or moved by key. Then apply compression, fixed shortlist conventions, and range clamps.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(M\\in I_b\\), the block-clean calculation replaces key comparisons by exact equality recursively throughout block \\(b\\), using actual algorithms below \\(M_b\\), with the same environments and fixed structural conventions.</p><p class=\"li\">The analytical access contracts are those of Proposition 3. Their deterministic implementation is established separately.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every allowed task and its precision \\(p\\),\n\\[\nd_{u,a}(\\overline H,H)\\le C_b2^{-p}\n\\]\nfor all actual row outputs, pre-incoming scores, and support witnesses. Clipped vectors satisfy the corresponding ordinary bound. In particular,\n\\[\n\\frac1N\\mathbb E_\\sigma\\sum_{x\\in V_l}\n\\frac{X_u(x)}u|\\overline W_l(x)-W_l(x)|^s\n\\le C_b^s2^{-sp}.\n\\]\nThis is original Theorem 9.8.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 7.7 adds actual–clean discrepancy to Proposition 6's spare clean bound. The induction includes every required row function, witness, and vector. Inactive bounds use fixed bounded ranges and supports. Taking the \\(s\\)-th power of the reward vector bound gives the displayed integrated moment estimate.</p><p>The access rule here is semantic in each task's effective environment. It does not assert that an adaptively selected ancestral transformation leaves a uniform conditional law. Fixed-variant changes of variables and the explicit clean-block argument are the uses justified in the proof.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.1", "orig": "Lemma 9.1, “Short fingerprints.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For endpoint encodings of length \\(L\\le c_{\\mathrm{ID}}B\\), there is a uniform map \\( k:\\{\\text{endpoint IDs}\\}\\to\\{1,\\ldots,B\\} \\) using \\(O(\\log B)\\) independent fair bits such that for distinct fixed \\(v,w\\) …", "statement": "<p>For endpoint encodings of length \\(L\\le c_{\\mathrm{ID}}B\\), there is a uniform map\n\\[\nk:\\{\\text{endpoint IDs}\\}\\to\\{1,\\ldots,B\\}\n\\]\nusing \\(O(\\log B)\\) independent fair bits such that for distinct fixed \\(v,w\\),\n\\[\n\\Pr[k(v)=k(w)]\\le C/B\\le CB^{-1/2}.\n\\]\nEvaluation at a presented endpoint needs \\(O(\\log B)\\) arithmetic scratch beyond access to its encoding.</p>", "proof": "<p>Write \\(P_v(T)=\\sum_{i=0}^{L-1}v_iT^i\\). Choose prime \\(B^3\\le q\\le2B^3\\), so \\(L&lt;q\\), and let \\(\\ell=\\lceil\\log q\\rceil\\). Reduce three independent uniform \\(\\ell\\)-bit integers modulo \\(q\\), obtaining \\(r,\\alpha,\\beta\\); each residue probability is at most \\(2/q\\).</p><p>Partition \\(\\mathbb F_q\\)'s standard residues into \\(B\\) consecutive nearly equal parts by \\(\\tau\\), and put\n\\[\nk(v)=\\tau(\\alpha P_v(r)+\\beta).\n\\]\nThe nonzero polynomial \\(P_v-P_w\\) has at most \\(L-1\\) roots, so its evaluation collision probability is at most \\(2(L-1)/q\\).</p><p>For distinct evaluations, \\((\\alpha,\\beta)\\mapsto(\\alpha z+\\beta,\\alpha z'+\\beta)\\) is bijective. Under uniform parameters, the bucket collision probability is\n\\[\nq^{-2}\\sum_j|\\tau^{-1}(j)|^2\\le1/B+1/q.\n\\]\nThe actual parameter law is at most four times uniform. Summing gives \\(O(1/B)\\). Horner evaluation and field arithmetic use \\(O(\\log B)\\) scratch. Prime search by trial division has that space bound.</p><p>There are \\(O(1+B/h_*)\\) salts, hence total salt storage\n\\[\nO((1+B/h_*)(\\log B+H))=O(B).\n\\]\nRaw fair-bit strings, not their reduced parameters, define the distribution.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.2", "orig": "Lemma 9.2, “Independence of the clean block.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Every clean value and structural choice in block \\(b\\) is independent of its effective block-\\(b\\) salt and later salts.", "statement": "<p>Every clean value and structural choice in block \\(b\\) is independent of its effective block-\\(b\\) salt and later salts. Immediately before the first group with \\(d\\le2h_*\\), actual and clean states agree, are independent of that salt, and have stored-list size \\(2^{O(h_*)}\\).</p><p>The deterministic slice-mean hypothesis of Proposition 5 holds for clean calculations.</p>", "proof": "<p>Induct on budget and the prescribed operation order. Lower-block algorithms ignore present and later salts by the access rule; same-block clean children do so inductively. Candidate tests, exact grouping, compression, exposed classes, port orders, scores, incoming ranks, and separate column queries use only these operands and fixed descriptor/tie conventions. The assertion concerns current effective coordinates; same-block transformations freeze present and later salts.</p><p>For \\(d&gt;2h_*\\), every child budget is below \\(M_b\\), so both calculations use identical lower-block children and exact comparisons. Their boundary states agree. The last larger dyadic slack is at most \\(4h_*\\), giving list size \\(2^{O(h_*)}\\); without such a group the list is empty.</p><p>Clean sample functions depend only on rank channels and earlier salts, exactly the variables varied by their conditional walks. Uniform slice averaging therefore leaves deterministic pairwise or vertex means; the second channel retains only its prescribed narrower mask. This proves the averaging hypothesis, without independence among different child errors.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.3", "orig": "Lemma 9.3, “Size and independence of the envelope.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For a retained row \\(x\\), condition on \\(\\mathcal G_b\\), all effective coordinates except the present salt.", "statement": "<p>For a retained row \\(x\\), condition on \\(\\mathcal G_b\\), all effective coordinates except the present salt. Let \\(E_x\\) contain:</p><p class=\"li\">the full boundary list, including stored exact-default slots;</p><p class=\"li\">for every small group and fixed immediate variant, all direct clean witness endpoints;</p><p class=\"li\">for every two-factor path, all right clean witness endpoints at midpoints in the left clean witness support;</p><p class=\"li\">applicable local base endpoints.</p><p>Set \\(E_x=\\varnothing\\) off the source domain. Then \\(E_x\\) is independent of the present salt, has size at most \\(2^{C_Eh_*}\\), and contains all clean nondefault representatives in small groups. Consequently,\n\\[\n\\Pr[\\text{distinct IDs in }E_x\\text{ collide}\\mid\\mathcal G_b]\n\\le C2^{2C_Eh_*}B^{-1/2}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Fixed variants leave the present salt unchanged and are independent of it. Lemma 7.2 makes all their clean witness supports measurable in \\(\\mathcal G_b\\). Each direct or two-factor witness contributes a bounded number of endpoints per descriptor. There are \\(2^{O(d)}\\) descriptors at slack \\(d\\), and \\(d\\le2h_*\\). Adding the boundary list gives the size bound.</p><p>Clean exposed ports lie inside these witnesses. Exact updates can create nondefault positions only at the included endpoints; compression sends untouched default positions to the new default. Extra clean default-valued list slots may be omitted when describing the full array. The conditional collision bound is a pair union bound using Lemma 7.1.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.4", "orig": "Lemma 9.4, “Charging an escaped candidate.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(F_x\\) be the event that some valid actual small-group candidate, even of zero increment, has endpoint outside \\(E_x\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(F_x\\) be the event that some valid actual small-group candidate, even of zero increment, has endpoint outside \\(E_x\\). Suppose budget-\\(m\\) actual–clean child discrepancies are at most \\(2^{-p_m}\\lambda_m\\).</p><p>The contribution of \\(F_x\\) to any bounded final pre-incoming row output has norm at most\n\\[\n2^{-p}\n\\sum_{\\substack{d\\le2h_*\\\\m\\in I_b,\\ m\\le M-d}}\n2^{Cd}\\lambda_m,\n\\]\nincluding the fixed dependency types and descriptor multiplicities.</p>", "proof": "<p>An escaped direct path has an actual port outside its clean witness support. A two-factor escape has either an offending left port or an offending right port at a clean-witness midpoint. At that port the actual witness is one and clean witness zero, so its event indicator is at most their root discrepancy to the \\(s\\)-th power. This includes exposed zero-weight ports.</p><p>Bounded output range and support charge the entire row failure to \\(1_{F_x}\\). Union-bound over paths. For a right offending port, after a fixed variant's measure-preserving change of variables, retain source, midpoint, and child endpoint memberships:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&amp;\\frac1N\\mathbb E\\sum_x\\frac{X_u(x)}u\n\\sum_{z:(x,z)\\in P_L^{\\mathrm{ac}}}Z_f(z)\n\\sum_{y:Y_{a'}(y)=1}|e_R(z,y)|^s\\\\\n&amp;\\qquad\\le K_c\\frac fu\\,\nd_{f,a'}(W_R^{\\mathrm{ac}},W_R^{\\mathrm{cl}})^s.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nOnly the actual left exposed relation's incoming cap is used. Its right tuple is independent of \\(x\\); otherwise the elimination would not compare one common discrepancy.</p><p>Direct and left offenses use their child row metrics; bounded right row degree costs a constant. Surplus narrower terminal masks can be discarded, avoiding a \\(\\pi_a/\\pi_t\\) penalty. The transformed source hit must remain until change of variables and incoming summation.</p><p>At slack \\(d\\), variants, scales, and descriptor counts cost \\(2^{O(d)}\\). Precision gains \\(\\frac12\\log(f/u)\\), absorbing \\((f/u)^{1/s}\\). Lower-block children are identical and contribute zero. Taking roots and summing gives the assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.5", "orig": "Lemma 9.5, “Only increments are aliased.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "On \\(F_x^c\\), let \\(\\Delta^{\\mathrm{ac}}(y)\\), \\(\\Delta^{\\mathrm{cl}}(y)\\) be the true-ID sums of a small group's increments, and let \\(k_d\\le2^{Cd}\\) bound immediate descriptors.", "statement": "<p>On \\(F_x^c\\), let \\(\\Delta^{\\mathrm{ac}}(y)\\), \\(\\Delta^{\\mathrm{cl}}(y)\\) be the true-ID sums of a small group's increments, and let \\(k_d\\le2^{Cd}\\) bound immediate descriptors. Let \\(C_x\\) be collision on \\(E_x\\).</p><p>In both channels, the sup discrepancy between actual bucket-distributed increment and clean true-ID increment is at most\n\\[\n(k_d+1)\\left(\n\\sup_y|\\Delta^{\\mathrm{ac}}(y)-\\Delta^{\\mathrm{cl}}(y)|\n+1_{C_x}\\sup_y|\\Delta^{\\mathrm{cl}}(y)|\n\\right).\n\\]\nThe prior full-array discrepancy is added with coefficient one.</p>", "proof": "<p>With distinct envelope keys, the rule gives exactly actual true-ID increments. With collision, any updated representative receives increments from at most \\(k_d\\) actual addresses; its magnitude is at most \\(k_d\\sup|\\Delta^{\\mathrm{ac}}|\\). Subtracting the clean increment costs at most \\(\\sup|\\Delta^{\\mathrm{cl}}|\\). Use\n\\[\n\\sup|\\Delta^{\\mathrm{ac}}|\n\\le\\sup|\\Delta^{\\mathrm{ac}}-\\Delta^{\\mathrm{cl}}|\n+\\sup|\\Delta^{\\mathrm{cl}}|.\n\\]\nThis covers duplication at old representatives and missing increments at unchosen new ones.</p><p>Old values are neither combined nor moved. New representatives start at the old default. Thus only increments are aliased, with no collision term proportional to an old value.</p><p>Within each paired difference, combine equal actual endpoints before measuring clean height. Outer tests are the same for the two budgets; each factor relation enumerates each pair once, with absence contributing zero. Subtracting the same true-atom term preserves cancellation. Summing paired bounds remains valid when one bucket representative is selected across several layers.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.6", "orig": "Lemma 9.6, “Direct cost of collisions.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The direct collision contribution to each final pre-incoming output has norm at most \\( 2^{-p}2^{Ch_*}B^{-1/(2s)}. \\) All other contributions on \\(F_x^c\\) are bounded by actual–clean child discrepancy norms with …", "statement": "<p>The direct collision contribution to each final pre-incoming output has norm at most\n\\[\n2^{-p}2^{Ch_*}B^{-1/(2s)}.\n\\]\nAll other contributions on \\(F_x^c\\) are bounded by actual–clean child discrepancy norms with amplification at most \\(2^{Cd}\\) at slack \\(d\\). The constant \\(C\\) is fixed before choosing the block-coupling constants.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(\\Delta_d^{\\mathrm{cl},\\nu}\\) be the complete clean true-ID increment, including any exact base term in that group. Proposition 5's absolute-difference estimate, applied to fixed variants and then summed crudely, gives\n\\[\n\\left\\|\\max_\\nu\\sup_y|\\Delta_d^{\\mathrm{cl},\\nu}(x,y)|\\right\\|_{\\mathrm{row},u;s}\n\\le2^{-p+O(d)}.\n\\]\nEqual endpoints are combined before this estimate. If a base term lies in a small first group, \\(M=O(d)\\), giving the same crude bound.</p><p>Compression propagation is polynomial in \\(d+1\\); common clamps are nonexpansive. Choose fixed \\(C_P,A_P\\) and define\n\\[\nH_x=C_P\\sum_{d\\le2h_*}(d+1)^{A_P}(k_d+1)\n\\max_\\nu\\sup_y|\\Delta_d^{\\mathrm{cl},\\nu}(x,y)|.\n\\]\nThen \\(\\|H\\|_{\\mathrm{row},u;s}\\le2^{-p+C_0h_*}\\). Both \\(H_x,E_x\\) are \\(\\mathcal G_b\\)-measurable. Therefore\n\\[\n\\mathbb E[1_{C_x}H_x^s\\mid\\mathcal G_b]\n\\le C2^{2C_Eh_*}B^{-1/2}H_x^s.\n\\]\nIntegrate and take roots. Do not condition on the salt-dependent stay-inside event: the error there is merely bounded by \\(1_{C_x}H_x\\) and integrated over the whole space.</p><p>The noncollision terms of Lemma 7.5 compare unaliased child sums. Bounded-factor splitting, row and incoming caps, precision gains, fixed-variant changes of variables, and compression propagation give \\(2^{Cd}\\) amplification, without assumptions of independent child errors or key success.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.7", "orig": "Proposition 9.7, “Uniform block coupling.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The fixed constants can be chosen so that at every budget \\(M\\in I_b\\), every prescribed actual–clean discrepancy, including scores, witnesses, and ordinary vector metrics, is at most …", "statement": "<p>The fixed constants can be chosen so that at every budget \\(M\\in I_b\\), every prescribed actual–clean discrepancy, including scores, witnesses, and ordinary vector metrics, is at most\n\\[\n2^{-p}\\lambda_M,\\qquad\n\\lambda_M=2^{-D_1h_*+D_2(M-M_b)}.\n\\]\nCombining this with the clean reserve proves the actual target bounds.</p>", "proof": "<p>Use strong induction on budget, simultaneously for actual target bounds, clean target bounds, and coupling. Inactive outputs agree. Lower-block children are identical within the coupling and have established actual errors; smaller same-block clean children have clean errors. Lemma 7.2 and Proposition 6 therefore give the clean reserve.</p><p>Lemmas 7.4–7.6 control pre-incoming coupling. Assembly uses Proposition 3's bounded incoming supports and root-Lipschitz gates; separate column calls have a fixed positive budget drop and precision-paid \\(f/u\\). Vector sums use the same crude comparisons without endpoint grouping.</p><p>For \\(m\\le M-d\\) in the same block,\n\\[\n\\lambda_m\\le\\lambda_M2^{-D_2d}.\n\\]\nAll child charges are bounded by\n\\[\n2^{-p}\\lambda_M\\sum_{d=1}^{2h_*}2^{-(D_2-C)d}.\n\\]\nChoose \\(D_2&gt;C\\) to make the infinite sum at most \\(1/4\\), then \\(D_1&gt;D_2+1\\), then rational \\(\\epsilon\\) so small that\n\\[\n1/(2s\\epsilon)&gt;C+D_1+2.\n\\]\nSince \\(h_*\\le\\epsilon\\log B\\), the direct collision charge is at most \\(\\frac14\\,2^{-p}2^{-D_1h_*}\\), after fixed minimum-size padding. Both charges fit the induction.</p><p>Also \\(\\lambda_M\\le2^{-(D_1-D_2)h_*}\\), uniformly small enough to add to the \\(C_b2^{-p}/2\\) clean reserve. Constants are fixed in this order: hierarchy and atom thresholds; precision and cutoff; compression and walks; clamps and crude exponents; then \\(D_2,D_1,\\epsilon\\). Later stage-size, top-budget, and field exponents do not change the crude exponent because gaps and immediate counts are uniform.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Every normalized recipe value has a deterministic exact \\(\\mathcal A\\)-stream.", "statement": "<p>Uniform finite numerical interface.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Integer clocks \\(t,j\\ge0\\) designate digit places; they are distinct from statistical budgets and usage rates.</p><p class=\"li\">Normalize values into \\([-1,1]\\), separating known binary scales.</p><p class=\"li\">A stream has digits in \\(\\mathcal A=\\{-6,-5,\\ldots,6\\}\\) and represents\n\\[\nx=\\sum_{j\\ge0}x_j2^{-j}.\n\\]\nIts truncation error is at most \\(6\\,2^{-K}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Root parameters and operand interfaces are regenerable from outer records without operand or graph queries. Operand labels do not contain discarded vertex addresses.</p><p class=\"li\">A slack-\\(d\\) recipe, \\(d\\ge1\\), has a fixed number of stages of bounded arithmetic, fixed piecewise max/min/clip formulas, positive-interval reciprocals, indexed ranks, bounded sums, and known scales.</p><p class=\"li\">Every indexed domain has at most \\(2^{O(d)}\\) descriptors of length \\(O(d)\\). Structural domains and tests have fixed conventions independent of requested output precision.</p><p class=\"li\">Stage generation and gate indexing use \\(O(B+d+t)\\) shared scratch without operand calls. Non-reduction dependency depth is bounded independently of \\(d\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Public coefficient leaves have exact normalized digit procedures using this shared scratch and no recursive graph calls.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every normalized recipe value has a deterministic exact \\(\\mathcal A\\)-stream. Uniform constants \\(a,b,c\\) give, for each operand query at clock \\(j\\),\n\\[\nj\\le t+cd,\\qquad\nL_{\\mathrm{suspended}}\\le ad+b(t-j).\n\\]\nStructural leaves are requested at clock zero with suspended length at most \\(ad+bt\\). Intrinsic local length is \\(O(d+t)\\). Gate, digit, and descriptor addresses can be regenerated in shared scratch.</p><p>Public rational coefficients with \\(O(B)\\)-bit numerator and denominator require only shared \\(O(B)\\) scratch for \\(O(B)\\)-place requests. This is original Theorem 10.9.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas 8.3–8.8 construct deterministic approximation integers; Lemma 8.1 gives exact streams, and Lemma 8.2 evaluates their finite circuits with regenerated addresses. A primitive approximates its own exact recipe value, so numerical errors are not accumulated as statistical errors.</p><p>Choose one common clock coefficient \\(b\\). If a primitive originally has \\(b_0\\le b\\) and overshoot \\(j-t\\le c_0d\\), enlarge its structural coefficient by \\((b-b_0)c_0\\). Fixed stages and balanced reductions then telescope to \\(ad+b(t-j)\\). The same accounting stopped at an internal operation gives intrinsic \\(O(d+t)\\).</p><p>Structural tests have their own fixed clocks; increasing a surrounding output clock never changes a semantic domain, port order, or exact target value. Public \\(P/Q\\) digits use signed integer division of \\(2^rP\\) by \\(Q\\) in \\(O(B+r)\\) scratch with no recursive calls. Operand-dependent words crossing calls were instead explicitly charged, including reciprocal early prefixes.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.1", "orig": "Lemma 10.1, “Exact streams from approximation residues.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Suppose \\(|z|\\le1\\) and deterministic integers \\(N_t(z)\\) satisfy \\( |N_t(z)-2^tz|<2. \\) Define \\( z_0=N_0(z),\\qquad z_t=N_t(z)-2N_{t-1}(z)\\quad(t\\ge1). \\) Then \\(z_t\\in\\mathcal A\\), and these digits represent \\(z\\) …", "statement": "<p>Suppose \\(|z|\\le1\\) and deterministic integers \\(N_t(z)\\) satisfy\n\\[\n|N_t(z)-2^tz|&lt;2.\n\\]\nDefine\n\\[\nz_0=N_0(z),\\qquad z_t=N_t(z)-2N_{t-1}(z)\\quad(t\\ge1).\n\\]\nThen \\(z_t\\in\\mathcal A\\), and these digits represent \\(z\\) exactly. For any fixed integer \\(q\\ge4\\), the required residues of \\(N_t,N_{t-1}\\) modulo \\(2^q\\) determine each output digit.</p>", "proof": "<p>Write \\(e_t=N_t-2^tz\\). For \\(t&gt;0\\),\n\\[\nz_t=e_t-2e_{t-1},\\qquad |z_t|&lt;6,\n\\]\nand \\(|N_0|&lt;3\\). The partial sum through \\(T\\) is \\(2^{-T}N_T\\to z\\). Since \\(2^q&gt;12\\), reduction is injective on possible signed digits. Compute the residue difference and decode it by a fixed finite table. Different clocks may use different deterministic finite approximations; each \\(N_{t-1}\\) is evaluated by its own place convention.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.2", "orig": "Lemma 10.2, “Evaluation from regenerated gate addresses.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let a finite bounded-fan-in Boolean circuit have a specified output.", "statement": "<p>Let a finite bounded-fan-in Boolean circuit have a specified output. Gate types, predecessors, and input operand labels/places are computable from root parameters and a path of child choices using \\(O(B+t+d)\\) scratch and no operand or graph queries. Conditional gates evaluate their selector first and only the selected branch.</p><p>Depth-first evaluation retains \\(O(1)\\) bits per gate on the active path and no saved absolute gate address per gate. Above an operand input, suspended length is a constant times path length plus any explicitly retained root-local record.</p>", "proof": "<p>Save the next-child/return mode and any bounded pending Boolean input; at a conditional gate also save the selected branch. To locate a child, replay the saved path from the root in scratch. Discard temporary addresses and counters before an operand query and regenerate them later. On return combine the result and pop the local record. Acyclicity gives termination. This unfolds the circuit into a recomputed formula rather than storing previously evaluated gates.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.3", "orig": "Lemma 10.3, “Finite comparisons and bounded elementary operations.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Normalized pair averages, maxima, minima, and fixed binary shifts admit approximation integers satisfying Lemma 8.1.", "statement": "<p>Normalized pair averages, maxima, minima, and fixed binary shifts admit approximation integers satisfying Lemma 8.1. For fixed residue modulus,\n\\[\nj\\le t+C_0,\\qquad\nL_{\\mathrm{suspended}}\\le C_0+C_1(t-j),\n\\]\nand intrinsic local space is \\(O(t+1)\\). Fixed finite compositions and Boolean operations on returned digit codes have the same form.</p>", "proof": "<p>Compare truncations through \\(K=t+h\\), for fixed large \\(h\\). Difference digits have magnitude at most \\(12\\). Scan from place zero to \\(K\\), updating \\(p\\leftarrow2p+\\delta\\). Retain \\(p\\) exactly while \\(|p|\\le12\\), and thereafter an absorbing sign. Remaining finite suffix magnitude is less than \\(12\\) in current scale, so the sign is correct. A comparison path to place \\(j\\) has length \\(O(K-j+1)\\).</p><p>Let \\(v_t\\) be the operation on truncations and \\(N_t=\\lfloor2^tv_t\\rfloor\\). These operations are sup-norm \\(1\\)-Lipschitz; large fixed \\(h\\) gives error \\(&lt;3/2\\).</p><p>For \\(N_t\\bmod2^q\\), terms at place \\(j\\le t-q\\) vanish modulo \\(2^{q+h}\\); only a fixed number of low places remain. Averages use\n\\[\nN_t=\\left\\lfloor(A_K+B_K)/2^{h+1}\\right\\rfloor\n\\]\nand one additional place. Max/min first select by the comparison bit. Fixed shifts alter places by constants.</p><p>Circuit topology depends only on root clock and path, so Lemma 8.2 gives the record bounds. Finite compositions use one common clock coefficient; increase constant terms to compensate fixed overshoot, and telescope clock differences.</p><p>Binary addition uses generate/propagate pairs\n\\[\n(g_2,p_2)\\circ(g_1,p_1)\n=(g_2\\vee(p_2\\wedge g_1),p_2\\wedge p_1).\n\\]\nBalanced trees give carry depth \\(O(\\log(W+1))\\); adding \\(R\\) words has depth \\(O(\\log(R+1)\\log(W+1))\\). Carry-save counting of \\(R\\) bits has depth \\(O(\\log(R+1))\\). These indexed bounded-fan-in constructions are used below.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.4", "orig": "Lemma 10.4, “Multiplication.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Multiplication of normalized exact streams has all conclusions of Lemma 8.3, including suspended storage \\(O(1)+c(t-j)\\) at an original operand place \\(j\\).", "statement": "<p>Multiplication of normalized exact streams has all conclusions of Lemma 8.3, including suspended storage \\(O(1)+c(t-j)\\) at an original operand place \\(j\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix residue parameter \\(q\\). Partition ordered digit pairs into the zero band \\(\\min(i,j)=0\\) and bands\n\\[\nP_D=\\{(i,j):D\\le\\min(i,j)&lt;2D\\},\n\\quad D=1,2,4,\\ldots.\n\\]\nPut \\(h_0=h_\\circ\\), \\(h_D=h_\\circ+\\lfloor D/4\\rfloor\\). Retain pairs \\(i+j\\le t+h_D\\), and define integers\n\\[\nS_D=\\sum_{(i,j)\\in P_D,\\ i+j\\le t+h_D}\nx_iy_j2^{t+h_D-i-j}.\n\\]\nEach diagonal has at most \\(2D\\) pairs, or two in the zero band. Omitted terms in scale-\\(t\\) units are bounded by\n\\[\n2\\cdot6^2\\sum_{D\\in\\{0,1,2,4,\\ldots\\}}(D+1)2^{-h_D}.\n\\]\nChoose \\(h_\\circ\\) so this and internal-floor errors total below \\(1/2\\). Nonempty positive retained bands satisfy \\(D\\le\\frac47(t+h_\\circ)\\).</p><p>Starting at the last band, set\n\\[\nF_D=S_D+\\lfloor2^{h_D-h_{D^+}}F_{D^+}\\rfloor,\\qquad\nN_t=\\lfloor2^{-h_0}F_0\\rfloor.\n\\]\nEach internal floor contributes less than \\(2^{-h_D}\\) in final scale, and the last floor less than one, proving approximation error \\(&lt;2\\).</p><p>Compute residues modulo \\(2^{q+h_D}\\). Terms with \\(i+j\\le t-q\\) vanish, leaving only\n\\[\nt-q&lt;i+j\\le t+h_D.\n\\]\nThere are \\(O((D+1)^2)\\) candidate pairs. Signed floor shifts are valid on residues because, if \\(h'\\ge h\\) and \\(a-b=k2^{q+h'}\\),\n\\[\n\\left\\lfloor a/2^{h'-h}\\right\\rfloor-\n\\left\\lfloor b/2^{h'-h}\\right\\rfloor\n=k2^{q+h}.\n\\]</p><p>Enumerate pairs by smaller index, diagonal, and orientation, with invalid choices zero. Their offsets have \\(O(\\log(D+2))\\) bits. Constant-bit digit products are shifted into \\(O(D+1)\\)-bit modular words and added by balanced arithmetic. Band depth is \\(O(\\log^2(D+2))\\); cumulative depth through \\(D\\) is \\(O(\\log^3(D+2))\\le C(D+1)\\).</p><p>For an original input place \\(j\\) in a positive band, its partner is at least \\(D\\), so\n\\[\nj\\le t+h_\\circ-\\tfrac34D.\n\\]\nThe path depth is therefore bounded by \\(C_0+C_1(t-j)\\). The zero band has constant overshoot and depth. Regenerate all indices from the path and apply Lemma 8.2.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.5", "orig": "Lemma 10.5, “Reciprocal on a positive compact interval.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For fixed \\(0<a\\le b\\), a reciprocal on \\(x\\in[a,b]\\), after fixed known normalization, has the operand-clock, suspended-record, and intrinsic-space bounds of Lemma 8.3.", "statement": "<p>For fixed \\(0&lt;a\\le b\\), a reciprocal on \\(x\\in[a,b]\\), after fixed known normalization, has the operand-clock, suspended-record, and intrinsic-space bounds of Lemma 8.3. An \\(O(t+1)\\)-bit local operand-dependent word is retained only while querying original input places\n\\[\nj\\le\\lfloor t/2\\rfloor+O(1).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Normalize so the output is \\(z=1/(Cx)\\in[0,1]\\), with fixed power-of-two \\(C\\) making subsequent coefficients bounded. Put\n\\[\nk=\\lfloor t/2\\rfloor+c,\\quad x^0=x^{[k]},\\quad e=x-x^0,\n\\]\nwith fixed \\(c\\) large enough that \\(x^0\\in[a/2,2b]\\). Let\n\\[\n\\alpha=1/(Cx^0),\\qquad \\beta=-1/(C(x^0)^2).\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n\\frac1{Cx}=\\alpha+\\beta e+\\frac{e^2}{C(x^0)^2x}.\n\\]\nChoose \\(c\\) so \\(2^t|\\alpha+\\beta e-z|\\le1/4\\).</p><p>For this fixed original \\(t\\), \\(\\alpha,\\beta\\) are exact rationals. The suffix stream for \\(e\\) is zero through \\(k\\), then uses original digits. Construct the clipped linear expression \\(\\widehat T_t\\) by multiplication, averaging, fixed clips, and shifts, with normalized intermediates. Let\n\\[\nN_t(z)=\\left\\lfloor P_{t+3}(\\widehat T_t)/8\\right\\rfloor,\n\\]\nwhere \\(P_{t+3}\\) is its approximation integer. Its error is\n\\[\n&lt;1+2/8+1/4=3/2.\n\\]</p><p>Form \\(x^0\\)'s numerator by querying places through \\(k\\), retaining its \\(O(k+1)\\)-bit prefix and counter. Coefficient digits are then computed by integer squaring and division in \\(O(k+r+1)\\) bits without further operand calls. At each original-input query during prefix formation, \\(j\\le t/2+O(1)\\), so\n\\[\nt+1\\le2(t-j)+O(1).\n\\]\nThis pays the entire early word and enclosing path.</p><p>On a late suffix branch, the coefficient word is absent: coefficient leaves return bounded digit bits and release their words before the suffix is queried. Repeated requests rebuild the prefix. Thus late queries retain only the clock-decrement-bounded path and pending bits. These operand-dependent coefficients are not public coefficient leaves.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.6", "orig": "Lemma 10.6, “Indexed finite ranks.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Fix \\(\\kappa>0,d\\ge1\\).", "statement": "<p>Fix \\(\\kappa&gt;0,d\\ge1\\). Let at most \\(2^{\\kappa d}\\) normalized values be indexed by \\(\\kappa d\\)-bit descriptors, with public semantic length \\(n\\). An order statistic has an \\(O(d)\\)-bit index in \\(\\{1,\\ldots,n\\}\\); out-of-range requests return a prescribed normalized constant. Invalid descriptors have prescribed dummy values selected by structural predicates.</p><p>The rank admits approximation integers of error \\(&lt;2\\), with\n\\[\nj\\le t+cd,\\qquad\nL_{\\mathrm{suspended}}\\le ad+b(t-j).\n\\]\nStructural validity leaves have clock zero and suspended length \\(O(d+t)\\). Intrinsic length is \\(O(d+t)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Convert the \\(k\\)-th largest to the \\((n-k+1)\\)-st ascending rank before extra padding. Pad to a power-of-two domain with \\(+1\\) entries; the first \\(n\\) ascending ranks are unchanged. Invalid original descriptors first select their defined dummy branch.</p><p>Truncate valid values through \\(K=t+h\\). Compare every pair, breaking ties by descriptor index. For each descriptor count predecessors, compare that count to the requested rank minus one, and select the unique descriptor's low residue bits. The rank is sup-norm Lipschitz, so uniform truncation error \\(6\\,2^{-K}\\), followed by flooring, gives error \\(&lt;2\\).</p><p>Counts have \\(O(d)\\) bits. Carry-save counting and selection add \\(O(d)\\) Boolean depth above comparison paths \\(O(t-j)\\) or low-residue paths of constant clock distance. Validity paths have total depth \\(O(d+t)\\). All gate indices are regenerated from root and path without numerical queries. Lemma 8.2 gives the bounds.</p><p>Semantic baseline copies in compression ranks are ordinary digit operands, selected structurally before querying their streams; absent real ports are not substituted for baseline values.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.7", "orig": "Lemma 10.7, “Bounded sums and known scales.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Suppose an immediate descriptor domain has at most \\(2^{\\kappa d}\\) terms, binary scale exponents have magnitude at most \\(\\kappa d\\), and known intermediate ranges are bounded by \\(2^{\\kappa d}\\).", "statement": "<p>Suppose an immediate descriptor domain has at most \\(2^{\\kappa d}\\) terms, binary scale exponents have magnitude at most \\(\\kappa d\\), and known intermediate ranges are bounded by \\(2^{\\kappa d}\\). Empty sums are zero; averages have public nonempty domains.</p><p>Balanced sums or averages, known rescalings, and bounded clipping satisfy Lemma 8.6's clock and suspended-record bounds and have intrinsic space \\(O(d+t)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Normalize terms by one known power-of-two bound and pad to \\(2^r\\) terms, \\(r=O(d)\\). A complete pair-average tree stays normalized. Its path has \\(O(d)\\) elementary operations; choose common clock coefficients and telescope their differences. Restore the normalization and, for sums, padding count in one \\(O(d)\\)-magnitude shift. For averages multiply by the public factor \\(2^r/m\\in[1,2)\\) using minimal padding.</p><p>For shift \\(2^v\\), truncate at \\(K=\\max(0,t+v+h)\\). If the shifted value is not known normalized, clip the scaled truncation before flooring, rather than exporting an unbounded normalized stream. Low residue uses places near \\(t+v\\); clipping comparisons use the corresponding known scaled endpoints. Their path cost is at most \\(O(d)+O(t-j)\\). If \\(t+v+h&lt;0\\), zero already approximates sufficiently and no operand is needed. Public nonbinary coefficients use normalized coefficient streams and bounded multiplication.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.8", "orig": "Lemma 10.8, “Finite buffered tests.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For \\(d\\ge1\\), suppose \\(s,u\\) have known magnitude at most \\(2^{\\kappa d}\\) and \\(u\\ge\\rho\\ge2^{-\\kappa d}\\).", "statement": "<p>For \\(d\\ge1\\), suppose \\(s,u\\) have known magnitude at most \\(2^{\\kappa d}\\) and \\(u\\ge\\rho\\ge2^{-\\kappa d}\\). A deterministic finite test accepts every \\(s&gt;2u\\) and accepts only \\(s&gt;u\\). It uses \\(O(d)\\)-place normalized queries and \\(O(d)\\) intrinsic and suspended local length, independently of later output clocks.</p>", "proof": "<p>Compute rational \\(\\widetilde s,\\widetilde u\\) with errors at most \\(\\rho/16\\), and accept exactly when\n\\[\n\\widetilde s&gt;\\tfrac32\\widetilde u\n\\]\nwith fixed equality convention. For \\(s&gt;2u\\), the difference is \\(&gt;u/2-5\\rho/32&gt;0\\); for \\(s\\le u\\), it is \\(\\le-u/2+5\\rho/32&lt;0\\). Required normalized precision is \\(O(d)\\). Structural starting clock is zero; its \\(O(d)\\) allowance pays operand overshoot and the negative clock-difference term.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "All actual reachable table, vector, and prefix interfaces have uniform deterministic implementations, total on every environment, independent of incoming scratch and catalytic contents.", "statement": "<p>Uniform restoring controller and total space bound.</p><p>Original headline label: Theorem 11.1, “Realization of the estimator interfaces.”</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix construction constants, input, master environment, and admissible \\(M_{\\mathrm{top}}=O(B)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A task tuple specifies the whole table: type, stage, budget, rates, roles, suffixes, and effective environment. An ancestral generator/wrap descriptor derives the environment but is not an extra semantic table parameter.</p><p class=\"li\">Shared facilities are one mutable vertex cursor, input and top parameters, master environment, one \\(B\\)-bit catalytic vector, and reusable scratch. Scratch contains no data that must survive a recursive graph call.</p><p class=\"li\">A restoring test returns one bit; a quantity query returns one bounded digit. Both restore cursor and catalyst.</p><p class=\"li\">A terminal motion ends at an entry's endpoint with no return address. An excursion visits it for a pure nonrecursive local action and returns. A paired motion returns a bounded inverse port.</p><p class=\"li\">Invalid or hole ports have total restoring existence tests, zero quantity streams, and failing motions that leave cursor and catalyst unchanged. Rank baseline and padding values remain separately prescribed.</p><p class=\"li\">Original Definition 11.2: a regenerable record is a delimited word of finite tags, immediate choices, offsets, numerical paths, pending bits, and permitted early words; root parameters and records recover descendant tuples and clocks in scratch without graph calls or a discarded cursor.</p><p class=\"li\">Original Definition 11.3: a raw allowance certificate assigns \\(w\\ge1,t\\ge0\\) with, at a lower boundary \\((w',j)\\),\n\\[\nw'&lt;w,\\quad j-t\\le a'(w-w'),\\quad\n\\ell\\le a_0(w-w')+b_0(t-j).\n\\]\nThe raw caller excludes callee working states; intrinsic raw length is at most \\(a_0w+b_0t\\). Structural starting clocks are zero.</p><p class=\"li\">Terminal program states contain cursor and explicit terminal controllers. Restoring numerical queries and lower paired motions are opaque transitions, not interior graph states.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>All actual reachable table, vector, and prefix interfaces have uniform deterministic implementations, total on every environment, independent of incoming scratch and catalytic contents. They compute their defined values and fixed structural choices, realize terminal endpoints and paired correction maps, and compute incoming zero-padded ranks.</p><p>For top numerical clock \\(t\\), total suspended records are \\(O(M_{\\mathrm{top}}+t+1)\\), descendant clocks are \\(O(M_{\\mathrm{top}}+t+1)\\), and shared facilities use \\(O(B+M_{\\mathrm{top}}+t+1)\\) space. Thus \\(t=O(B)\\) gives total \\(O(B)\\) work space. No second unrestricted vertex address survives recursive graph operations.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose \\(S\\) above the finite number of strata from Lemma 9.8. Choose fixed integers \\(K_0&gt;S+1\\) and \\(K_1&gt;2(K_0+S+1)\\), enlarged for local constants. At budget \\(M\\), slack \\(d\\), and stratum \\(s_0\\), assign\n\\[\nw=K_1(1+M)-(K_0+s_0)d.\n\\]\nFinal wrappers use \\(d=1\\); nontrivial prefixes have \\(1\\le d\\le M\\). Same-slack descent decreases allowance by at least \\(d\\); descent to slack \\(2d\\) by at least \\((K_0-S)d\\); descent to budget \\(m\\le M-d\\) by at least \\((K_1-K_0-S)d\\). Allowances stay positive and \\(O(1+M)\\). Lemma 9.8 therefore gives the raw certificate, including structural test clocks and rollback.</p><p>Construct all admissible operation instances in increasing allowance, including synthetic predecessor probes. Bottom operations are local base ports, hash/rank evaluation, public rational arithmetic, and zero tasks. Lower total procedures make restoring/numerical recipes finite and well defined. Lemmas 9.6–9.7 implement ordered visits and catalyst-independent comparisons. Lemma 9.9 supplies replay and salt restrictions. Apply Lemma 9.2 only after the raw certificate is proved: designated executions pass guards, while artificial states invoke only safe canonical lower entries.</p><p>Lemmas 9.3–9.5 then implement cap and inverse-rank wrappers on finite success-root trees, regardless of cycles elsewhere. Canonical initial checks ensure they count exactly the intended class entries. One uniform interpreter for a fixed finite instruction family implements these runtime-parameterized procedures; no stored transition graphs or per-budget program texts are needed.</p><p>Fix enlarged physical constants \\(A,D\\) from Lemma 9.2, and write\n\\[\n\\Phi(w,t)=Aw+Dt,\\qquad \\Psi(w,t)=t+a'w.\n\\]\nA physical segment above a lower query contributes at most\n\\[\n\\Phi(w_i,t_i)-\\Phi(w_{i+1},t_{i+1}),\n\\]\nincluding fixed local raw copies, not copied older physical segments. The bottom contributes at most \\(\\Phi(w_r,t_r)+O(1)\\). Thus all live local records telescope to\n\\[\n\\sum_{i&lt;r}(\\Phi_i-\\Phi_{i+1})+\\Phi_r+O(1)\n=Aw_{\\mathrm{top}}+Dt_{\\mathrm{top}}+O(1).\n\\]\nThe clock overshoot inequality makes \\(\\Psi\\) nonincreasing, so every descendant clock is \\(O(w_{\\mathrm{top}}+t_{\\mathrm{top}})\\).</p><p>Environment, cursor, catalyst, scratch, and global scans are shared once. Operand-dependent words crossing calls are charged locally; public scratch computations make no recursive calls. Physical input reads regenerate coordinates in a pure routine and stay between real endmarkers. Total space is therefore \\(O(B+M_{\\mathrm{top}}+t_{\\mathrm{top}}+1)\\), including inaccurate environments and guarded predecessor probes.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.1", "orig": "Lemma 9.9, “Restoring equality test.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(V\\in\\{0,1\\}^B\\) have arbitrary incoming contents.", "statement": "<p>Let \\(V\\in\\{0,1\\}^B\\) have arbitrary incoming contents. Suppose excursions to two deterministic endpoints restore the cursor, have paths and results independent of incoming \\(V\\), and may use nested procedures that restore \\(V\\).</p><p>Four excursions suffice to xor \\(1_{\\{k(v)=k(w)\\}}\\) into a local result bit, restore \\(V\\), and retain only constant local data.</p>", "proof": "<p>Flip \\(V_{k(v)}\\); xor \\(V_{k(w)}\\) into the result; flip \\(V_{k(v)}\\) again; xor \\(V_{k(w)}\\) again. The two reads are respectively\n\\[\nV^0_{k(w)}\\oplus1_{\\{k(v)=k(w)\\}},\\qquad V^0_{k(w)}.\n\\]\nTheir xor gives equality and restores the vector.</p><p>Induct on the well-founded procedure order. Nested calls restore arbitrary incoming contents and return independent results, so repeated outer endpoint visits are identical. The result bit does not select paths; terminal key evaluation is pure and nonrecursive. Only phase and result survive, while the key index is recomputed at each visit.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.2", "orig": "Lemma 11.4, “Guarded local graph.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Suppose a terminal recipe has regenerable records and raw certificates on designated executions.", "statement": "<p>Suppose a terminal recipe has regenerable records and raw certificates on designated executions. Its instructions are:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. Bounded local edits, with marked erasures and incremental pushes/pops.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. Pure or lower restoring branches changing only bounded local data.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. Lower partial paired cursor steps with regenerated tuples and bounded tokens.</p><p class=\"li num\">4. Explicit lower terminal delegations.</p><p>Assume opaque queries are already total on admissible arguments, catalyst-independent, restoring, and safe on invalid ports.</p><p>There is a finite outdegree-at-most-one guarded graph, determined by the logical root tuple, whose designated executions are exactly the recipe's executions. Successes halt and are distinct from initiation tags. Predecessor cases and paired incidences use bounded case labels and one cursor. Before any opaque predecessor query, its bound is checked.</p><p>For a root \\((w,t)\\) and probed lower query \\((w',j)\\), all live local raw copies and metadata occupy at most\n\\[\nA(w-w')+D(t-j).\n\\]\nThe graph and guards are independent of the originating row, outer-stack contents, and catalyst.</p>", "proof": "<p>Potential states are cursor plus tagged delimited raw words. Pure guards check ranges, types, modes, descriptor recipes, salt permissions, intrinsic word lengths, and every ancestor-to-query raw bound along the local explicit chain. Unavailable instructions have no edge. Designated paths satisfy these checks by their raw certificates; the graph is finite and deterministic.</p><p>For local-edit predecessors, guess only bounded overwritten symbols, old modes, and head movements, then test forward. Marked erasure prevents forgotten arbitrary positions. For branches, repeat the canonical lower restoring query at unchanged cursor; fabricated historical flags never resume an internal continuation.</p><p>For paired-step predecessors, first form the bounded candidate local edit. Replay its records and check static recipe, allowance, clock, and salt conditions before movement, without a predecessor cursor. Regenerate the lower tuple and apply its inverse at the present cursor and output token. A successful inverse returns the actual prior token; compare any guess with it. Restore a probe by the paired forward map with that same tuple and token. Cursor-specific pure checks occur locally; any additional restoring domain test is a separate branch edge. Outgoing target-guard failures likewise roll back by the inverse.</p><p>Canonical reverse case codes remove duplicate predecessor descriptions using finite labels rather than retained addresses.</p><p>Adding raw bounds along the explicit chain telescopes to\n\\[\na_0\\Delta w+b_0\\Delta t,\\quad\n\\Delta w=w-w'&gt;0,\\quad \\Delta t=t-j\\ge-a'\\Delta w.\n\\]\nA fixed number \\(k\\) of edited or saved raw copies plus bounded metadata \\(h\\) is covered by choosing\n\\[\nD\\ge kb_0,\\qquad\nA\\ge ka_0+(D-kb_0)a'+h.\n\\]\nOnly this local raw chain is copied. Lower opaque procedures append new physical segments; their interiors and older outside segments are not copied into this graph. Active-copy tags select the proper replay chain. Thus the constants are enlarged once, not recursively at each nesting depth.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.3", "orig": "Lemma 11.5, “Inverse contour.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For a finite deterministic graph from Lemma 9.2 with a specified canonical success halt, its undirected component is a tree directed toward that halt.", "statement": "<p>For a finite deterministic graph from Lemma 9.2 with a specified canonical success halt, its undirected component is a tree directed toward that halt.</p><p>Mark the root sentinel and one distinguished successor incidence at every canonical initiation. Mutually inverse next-mark and previous-mark operations traverse these marks using current program state and bounded incidence control, without a saved root address or contour-length counter.</p>", "proof": "<p>An outdegree-at-most-one graph component containing a sink has neither a directed cycle nor another sink: a cycle cannot connect to a sink without some vertex needing a second outgoing edge. Hence the component is a finite tree directed to its halt.</p><p>Add a sentinel incidence at the root. Edge flip exchanges the incidences of each edge and fixes the sentinel; local cyclic rotation followed by flip gives one contour cycle. Inserting a leaf inserts its two incidences into the previous cycle, proving this inductively; an isolated root has only its sentinel.</p><p>Iterate contour successor or predecessor to the next mark. The finite contour contains the root mark, so this terminates. A success tag and sentinel token identify the root locally, without comparing its address with a saved one. This is the rotation–edge-flip contour used in the cited Cook–McKenzie traversal.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.4", "orig": "Lemma 11.6, “Capped incoming ports.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let a row class have a constant port alphabet, distinct entries per row, and a deterministic terminal program of Lemma 9.2 mapping valid row ports to endpoints.", "statement": "<p>Let a row class have a constant port alphabet, distinct entries per row, and a deterministic terminal program of Lemma 9.2 mapping valid row ports to endpoints.</p><p>For fixed \\(K_c\\), there are restoring tests and inverse paired motions for precisely those class entries whose endpoint has at most \\(K_c\\) incoming class entries. Inverse ports lie in \\(\\{1,\\ldots,K_c\\}\\). No additional endpoint address or unbalanced path is retained.</p>", "proof": "<p>Invalid canonical ports go to distinct failure executions. Initiations in a success component are exactly valid entries ending there.</p><p>From a valid initiation, move forward through marks toward the sentinel, stopping there or after \\(K_c\\) other initiations; restore by the bounded count of inverse jumps. Repeat backward. Seeing \\(K_c\\) other marks certifies excess. Otherwise, if the two other-mark counts are \\(a,b\\), the total is \\(1+a+b\\); accept when it is at most \\(K_c\\), and assign inverse rank \\(r=b+1\\). The forward motion reaches the sentinel in \\(a+1\\) jumps.</p><p>From an endpoint halt, count forward initiations until sentinel return or \\(K_c+1\\) marks. Restore before rejecting excess. If \\(n\\le K_c\\), a valid inverse rank \\(r\\) reaches the \\(r\\)-th initiation and returns its stored row port. Invalid ranks fail at the recovered sentinel; no preimages means \\(n=0\\).</p><p>Both directions use the same root tuple and contour order. Counts and tokens are constant-sized. Every failure restores the endpoint, and all lower queries restore the catalyst.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.5", "orig": "Lemma 11.7, “Incoming score rank.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let a constant-size forward class contain every positive entry of a bounded nonnegative score table, with distinct entries per row.", "statement": "<p>Let a constant-size forward class contain every positive entry of a bounded nonnegative score table, with distinct entries per row. Assume restoring pre-incoming score digits and the terminal interfaces above.</p><p>A fixed incoming \\(k\\)-th-largest value, zero-padded, has restoring exact redundant digits. Above a score query at place \\(j\\), output approximation clock \\(t\\) retains\n\\[\nO(1+(t-j)_+)\n\\]\nlocal numerical bits, with fixed overshoot \\(j\\le t+c\\), in addition to structural allowance costs. A full \\(O(t+1)\\)-bit score word is retained only over structural calls.</p>", "proof": "<p>Normalize scores to \\([0,1]\\), and choose fixed \\(c\\) so the truncation error is less than \\(2^{-t}\\). Define\n\\[\nq_t(v)=\\operatorname{clip}_{[0,2^t]}\n\\left(\\left\\lfloor2^tv^{[t+c]}\\right\\rfloor\\right).\n\\]\nThen \\(|q_t(v)-2^tv|&lt;2\\), and \\(q_t(0)=0\\). The covering class therefore suffices for the quantized column rank with zero padding.</p><p>Use a terminal scoring program whose initial check validates the full class and domain and whose success state contains endpoint cursor and \\(q_t(v)\\). Assemble the score from least to most significant digit. Before a place-\\(j\\) query, the partial suffix and carry occupy \\(O(1+t+c-j)\\) bits; its offset has the same bound. After assembly, flooring and clipping are pure. The full word survives only the final structural endpoint motion and clearing.</p><p>At the requested endpoint, initialize success value \\(q=2^t\\). Tour that component, counting initiations with saturation at fixed \\(k\\), continuing to sentinel even after saturation. If not enough, decrement the value field and tour the next component. This field is the loop variable, not an external parameter; during inverse traversal it is replaced by an earlier partial-score state rather than saved alongside it. Unique-halt recovery restores endpoint and score after each tour. At zero apply zero padding.</p><p>The selected quantized rank \\(N_t\\) has approximation error \\(&lt;2\\), since order statistics are uniformly Lipschitz. Retain its fixed residue bits, clear remaining local data without graph queries, and use Lemma 8.1. Each \\(N_{t-1}\\) uses its own quantization. All component tours and finite decreasing score loops terminate and restore the catalyst.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.6", "orig": "Lemma 11.8, “Ordered terminal paths and excursions.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Every required unbalanced estimator path has this order:", "statement": "<p>Every required unbalanced estimator path has this order:</p><p class=\"li\">restoring selection and validity tests;</p><p class=\"li\">local label maps and, when needed, a paired left correction move;</p><p class=\"li\">restoring midpoint tests;</p><p class=\"li\">at most one final terminal delegation;</p><p class=\"li\">only local label completion afterward.</p><p>Its endpoint excursion for a pure action has regenerable records and the same allowance/clock bounds up to fixed factors. No unbalanced spine is retained over a new high-cost endpoint query.</p>", "proof": "<p>A direct path is a base path, projected child path, or child row port. Projection saves a bounded source symbol and appends its prescribed destination symbol after delegation. Products use a paired correction left factor, retaining its short inverse token; right validity and digits are restoring at the midpoint, and the right terminal path is last. Old prefix ports delegate with no new endpoint graph query afterward.</p><p>A one-way continuation clears records after local completion. A visiting continuation retains each frame's immediate choices and inverse tokens, performs the pure endpoint action, undoes bounded completions and the last delegated spine, then crosses earlier paired inverses. Deeper records are cleared before earlier inverse calls. Thus rollback retains only the corresponding forward boundary's own frames and bounded phases.</p><p>Intrinsic constituent validity is checked before a visit. Product right validity uses the paired left edge and return, not its own terminal path. Outer wrap and slice tests use an earlier intrinsic excursion, preventing bucket/shortlist validity cycles. An owner tag regenerates the pure action's tuple, and the action result does not alter path or rollback.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.7", "orig": "Lemma 11.9, “Implemented endpoint comparison.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "At slack \\(d\\), comparing valid candidate or old-list endpoints is a restoring catalyst-independent test retaining \\(O(d)\\) structural control, besides lower visit costs, with no saved endpoint address.", "statement": "<p>At slack \\(d\\), comparing valid candidate or old-list endpoints is a restoring catalyst-independent test retaining \\(O(d)\\) structural control, besides lower visit costs, with no saved endpoint address. Exact versus fingerprint mode is independent of numerical output precision.</p>", "proof": "<p>Two immediate descriptors use \\(O(d)\\) bits. Lemma 9.6 supplies restoring visits. For \\(d&gt;2h_*\\), compare \\(O(B)\\) ID bits with a position counter of \\(O(\\log B)=O(d)\\) bits. Otherwise apply Lemma 9.1's four visits. Evaluate each key and catalyst-bit action only at the endpoint in shared scratch, retaining no key index.</p><p>By lower-operation induction, nested calls restore arbitrary catalyst contents and have independent answers. Outer paths ignore the accumulated result, so repeated visits agree. Mode depends only on public budget/block/slack data. The analytical clean calculation replaces the short test by exact equality; only the actual calculation requires the tight physical-space bound. Both comparisons are opaque restoring branches in program graphs.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.8", "orig": "Lemma 11.10, “Concrete recipe certificates.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Actual estimator operations have regenerable records and a fixed finite acyclic order of operation strata.", "statement": "<p>Actual estimator operations have regenerable records and a fixed finite acyclic order of operation strata. At prefix slack \\(d\\), suspended local records above an operand at clock \\(j\\) satisfy\n\\[\nc_{\\mathrm{str}}d+c_{\\mathrm{num}}(t-j),\n\\qquad j-t\\le c_{\\mathrm{dig}}d.\n\\]\nBeyond the fixed strata, calls go to the preceding prefix of slack \\(2d\\) or an estimator of budget at most \\(M-d\\). Final row and incoming wrappers use finitely many strata at \\(d=1\\).</p><p>Every designated terminal execution satisfies these raw certificates before graph guards are imposed.</p>", "proof": "<p>Immediate descriptors have \\(O(d)\\) bits and smaller-budget factors. Index checks are pure. Products cross paired left corrections for right tests/digits and return after each query; arithmetic requests separate factor digits, never a retained full left value over a late right query. Rate coefficients have logarithmic range \\(O(d)\\); the analytical \\(f/u\\) cost is not a numerical coefficient.</p><p>Separate intrinsic existence, intrinsic visits, outer validity, and outer-valid visits. Outer validity uses earlier constituent tests and intrinsic excursions; none tests its new bucket or shortlist. Terminal delegation is last.</p><p>The pool consists of old-list slots and unmerged new paths. First-representative and key-match predicates enumerate \\(2^{O(d)}\\) descriptors with \\(O(d)\\)-bit indices and counts, using only earlier paths. Bucket sums are balanced numerical reductions, not sequential high-precision accumulators.</p><p>Intermediate entries and flags precede ranks. Include required default copies in finite \\(2^{O(d)}\\) rank domains. Compression is a fixed arithmetic recipe with range \\(2^{O(d)}\\). Since \\(u_0\\ge2^{-c_1d}\\), a fixed \\(O(d)\\)-place buffered test selects every size \\(&gt;2u_0\\) and only sizes \\(&gt;u_0\\). Its precision never changes with output clock. Enumerating accepted descriptors remaps list ordinals with \\(O(d)\\)-bit control. Terminal list ports delegate through preceding prefixes instead of carrying long historical paths.</p><p>Every exported real-port value or motion freshly checks presence at canonical entry. A failed ordinal lookup returns no descriptor; rank operands separately choose entry, baseline, or padding. Final precursor and auxiliary classes use fixed-margin tests on completed raw arrays, before their own gate or cap. Endpoint membership uses earlier intrinsic/list excursions, not the class motion being defined. Synthetic historical validity flags are never certificates. Guards permit only total canonical wrappers.</p><p>A finite dependency refinement orders compressed entries, ordinal/compression selection, intermediate ranks, bucket entries/flags, representative predicates, comparisons, outer-valid visits, outer validity, intrinsic visits, constituent tests, factor digits, and motions. Loops remain local rather than one recursive frame per descriptor.</p><p>At final slack the list and port alphabet are constant. Pre-incoming score and normalized row formulas use completed raw arrays and positive clipped denominators. Cap and auxiliary score terminal programs validate full classes initially; their value, gate, and cap dependencies are separate. Lemmas 9.4–9.5 supply incoming wrappers. A correction value crosses its paired edge, retains only the bounded inverse port while requesting gate or lower-budget column digits, then returns. Detector trials and reward midpoint queries use the same pattern.</p><p>All numerical recipes satisfy Proposition 8: fixed polynomial switches, maxima, minima, variable clipping, reciprocals on \\([1/2,1]\\), finite ranks, and balanced sums. Eighth roots are only analytical regularity, not requested arithmetic. Public field, rate, and walk coefficients use shared scratch without recursive calls. Only explicitly charged early words or score suffixes exceed \\(O(d)\\) local control. Ordered terminal rollback satisfies the same boundary estimates. This establishes the raw certificate independently of guards.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.9", "orig": "Lemma 11.11, “Parameter replay and salt visibility.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The records of Lemma 9.8 are regenerable.", "statement": "<p>The records of Lemma 9.8 are regenerable. In their effective environments, block-\\(b\\) procedures ignore all later salt blocks in values, structural orders, graph universes, guards, and predecessor probes. Re-querying an entry or using paired ports in either direction uses the same effective table tuple.</p>", "proof": "<p>Replay from fixed top parameters along active delimited records. Task type, stage and budget offsets, scale offset, copy symbols, and role recipes determine descendant rates and suffixes. Projection appends its stored symbol before inherited suffix; midpoint/vector calls use their prescribed stage-rate tuples without old source addresses.</p><p>Averaging records specify endpoint-independent generator/wrap transformations, so effective environments can be regenerated before vertex tests. Final gates and column multipliers use their table tuple, not the ancestral descriptor that discovered an entry. An owner tag selects the appropriate ancestor tuple for a pure endpoint action.</p><p>Numerical paths regenerate gates and clocks by Proposition 8's index rules. Cursor-dependent selections were stored as bounded choices before movement, so replay does not repeat them at a lost cursor. A scratch scan retains only one current \\(O(B)\\)-bit absolute tuple and temporary integers; active-copy tags ignore saved inactive chains.</p><p>Only a new group comparison reads its own permitted salt. Walks change designated earlier free blocks and freeze current/later blocks. Fixed index orders, static bounds, and syntactic salt-index permissions apply also to synthetic predecessor records. They cannot increase budgets, bypass parent-child rules, or select internal continuations.</p><p>The assertion is about a function of its effective argument \\(\\eta\\). An ancestor may supply \\(\\eta=\\phi(\\sigma)\\), and physical replay of permitted coordinates may read master bits with different original indices. This does not grant access to later coordinates of \\(\\eta\\). A higher-owned pure endpoint callback remains outside the lower graph and cannot change its path or rollback.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 10", "orig": "Theorem 12.1, “Fixed inverse-polynomial accuracy.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is a uniform deterministic transducer outputting a dyadic \\(z_x\\in[0,1]\\) such that \\( |z_x-p_{\\mathcal M}(x)|\\le(n+2)^{-d}. \\) Its space and output length are \\(O(\\log(n+2))\\), and its running time is polynomial …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix a randomized machine \\(\\mathcal M\\) with polynomial worst-case running time and \\(O(\\log(n+2))\\) work space.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix an integer \\(d\\ge1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(p_{\\mathcal M}(x)\\) be its acceptance probability, with no decision-gap promise.</p><p class=\"li\">A transducer's output is one-way write-only and unavailable as work memory.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a uniform deterministic transducer outputting a dyadic \\(z_x\\in[0,1]\\) such that\n\\[\n|z_x-p_{\\mathcal M}(x)|\\le(n+2)^{-d}.\n\\]\nIts space and output length are \\(O(\\log(n+2))\\), and its running time is polynomial in \\(n+2\\). Constants may depend on \\(\\mathcal M,d\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>First fix constants in the paper's dependency order: channel dimensions; correction thresholds and finite iteration; copies and gate ladders; \\(K\\), then \\(J,R_*,K_c\\); precision/cutoff and prefix exponents; compression and walk constants; clamps, crude exponents, and fingerprint-block constants; controller strata and guards; finally top budget, field exponent, and minimum \\(B\\).</p><p>For a task of budget \\(M\\), reciprocal-rate logarithms are bounded by\n\\[\nHL+c(M_{\\mathrm{top}}-M)\n\\]\nfor fixed sufficiently large \\(c\\): a slack-\\(d\\) extension adds \\(O(d)\\) while losing at least \\(d\\) budget; stage resets are covered by \\(HL\\). Choose prime\n\\[\n2^{C_PB}\\le P\\le2^{C_PB+1}\n\\]\nabove all identifiers and rates. Bertrand's postulate and trial division give uniform \\(O(B)\\)-space, \\(2^{O(B)}\\)-time selection.</p><p>Apply Proposition 1. Choose \\(|V_0|\\le2^{C_0B}\\), and set\n\\[\nr=d\\lceil\\log(n+2)\\rceil,\\quad \\Delta=2^{-r},\\quad\nL=\\left\\lceil\\frac{C_0B+r+3}{H-1}\\right\\rceil.\n\\]\nAt the all-primary start \\(z\\), Proposition 2 gives\n\\[\n0\\le p_{\\mathcal M}(x)-W_L(z)\\le\\Delta/8.\n\\]\nFix \\(N=2^{C_NB}\\) bounding all stages. At the final reward task, \\(a=u=f=b_L\\), so\n\\[\np=M_{\\mathrm{top}}/K_{\\mathrm{prec}}-HL-A_*i_*.\n\\]\nChoose the linear coefficient of \\(M_{\\mathrm{top}}=O(B)\\) large enough that\n\\[\np\\ge r+3+\\frac{C_NB+\\lceil\\log C_{\\mathrm{tail}}\\rceil+3}{8},\n\\]\nwhere \\(C_{\\mathrm{tail}}\\ge(f/\\pi_f)C_b^8\\) uniformly. Proposition 7 and Lemma 10.1 then give\n\\[\n\\Pr[|\\overline W_L(z)-W_L(z)|&gt;\\Delta/8\\mid X_f(z)=1]\\le1/8.\n\\]</p><p>For each environment, Proposition 8 gives digits of one fixed estimator value, with structural choices independent of the requested digit. Put \\(K=r+6\\), compute\n\\[\nA_\\sigma=\\sum_{j=0}^Kw_j2^{K-j},\\quad\na_\\sigma=\\min(2^K,\\max(0,A_\\sigma)),\\quad\nv_\\sigma=a_\\sigma2^{-K}.\n\\]\nThe recurrence \\(A\\leftarrow2A+w_j\\) uses \\(O(K)\\) bits; truncation error is at most \\(6\\,2^{-K}&lt;\\Delta/8\\), and clipping cannot increase error relative to \\(W_L(z)\\). On more than three quarters of the conditional environments,\n\\[\n|v_\\sigma-p_{\\mathcal M}(x)|&lt;3\\Delta/8.\n\\]</p><p>Enumerate raw field-matrix encodings and original salt bit strings, retaining exactly frame-valid encodings with \\(X_f(z)=1\\). These equally weighted encodings reproduce the conditional law, including multiplicities of salts reducing to the same key parameters. A retained encoding exists because a frame exists and its independent constant column can force the indicated evaluation into the nonempty box. Encoding length is \\(O(B)\\), so there are at most \\(2^{O(B)}\\) possibilities.</p><p>Count retained encodings \\(E\\), set \\(h=\\lfloor E/2\\rfloor+1\\), and binary-search \\([0,2^K]\\) for the least \\(a\\) with at least \\(h\\) values \\(a_\\sigma\\le a\\). Every count is a fresh enumeration. The resulting upper median lies in the same good interval, so \\(z_x=a2^{-K}\\) has error \\(&lt;3\\Delta/8\\le(n+2)^{-d}\\).</p><p>Proposition 9 counts all query records, environment, cursor, catalyst, and scratch in \\(O(B)\\). The outer computation adds only a fixed number of \\(O(B)\\)-bit registers: enumeration state, start and parameters, counts, search endpoints, digit index, and numerator accumulator. These coexist with digit queries and are counted once.</p><p>Every digit query halts on every environment, uses \\(O(B)\\) space, and confines physical input heads. It has \\(2^{O(B)}\\) complete configurations; repeating one would cause a deterministic loop. Thus each query takes \\(2^{O(B)}\\) time. Multiplying by \\(2^{O(B)}\\) encodings and \\(O(K)\\) passes and digits remains \\(2^{O(B)}=(n+2)^{O(1)}\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.1", "orig": "Lemma 12.2, “Conditioning on one start.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Suppose \\(W\\in[0,1]^V\\) and \\( \\frac1N\\mathbb E\\sum_{v\\in V} \\frac{X_f(v)}f|\\overline W(v)-W(v)|^8 \\le C_b^82^{-8p}. \\) For a specified \\(z\\in V\\) and \\(\\eta>0\\) …", "statement": "<p>Suppose \\(W\\in[0,1]^V\\) and\n\\[\n\\frac1N\\mathbb E\\sum_{v\\in V}\n\\frac{X_f(v)}f|\\overline W(v)-W(v)|^8\n\\le C_b^82^{-8p}.\n\\]\nFor a specified \\(z\\in V\\) and \\(\\eta&gt;0\\),\n\\[\n\\mathbb E[|\\overline W(z)-W(z)|^8\\mid X_f(z)=1]\n\\le N\\frac f{\\pi_f}C_b^82^{-8p},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\Pr[|\\overline W(z)-W(z)|&gt;\\eta\\mid X_f(z)=1]\n\\le N\\frac f{\\pi_f}C_b^82^{-8p}\\eta^{-8}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Keep only the nonnegative summand at \\(z\\):\n\\[\n\\frac{\\pi_f}f\n\\mathbb E[|\\overline W(z)-W(z)|^8\\mid X_f(z)=1]\n\\le NC_b^82^{-8p}.\n\\]\nDivide by \\(\\pi_f/f&gt;0\\), then apply Markov's inequality. Selecting one coordinate costs the factor \\(N\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 11", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "From a source code and positive supplied bounds \\(a,b\\), finite syntactic operations construct a total deterministic simulation of \\(A\\) on a virtual padded input.", "statement": "<p>Fixed universal interpreter and syntactic specialization.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix a coding of finite transition lists using the field encoding \\(w\\mapsto1^{|w|}0w\\), with fixed nesting depth and explicitly listed state, alphabet, tape, and transition data.</p><p class=\"li\">A code \\(z\\) has length \\(d_{\\mathrm{code}}\\ge1\\), at most \\(d_{\\mathrm{code}}\\) work tapes, at most \\(d_{\\mathrm{code}}\\) fresh coin bits per interpreted transition, and IDs of length at most \\(d_{\\mathrm{code}}\\). Missing transitions reject; the first matching listed transition is used.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(E_{d_{\\mathrm{code}}}(x)\\) encode source symbols in fixed-width \\(d_{\\mathrm{code}}\\)-bit IDs.</p><p class=\"li\">Source conversion merely renames and lists transitions, retaining work writes, head movements, and the count of interpreted transitions. Any cached one-way random-tape bit and validity flag are finite-control data.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(U\\) be the fixed capped interpreter of Lemma 11.1.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a total deterministic separator \\(A\\) for the \\(2/3\\) versus \\(1/3\\) acceptance-probability thresholds of \\(U\\), supplied by Proposition 10 at exponent \\(3\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(B_n=\\lceil\\log(n+2)\\rceil\\). Fix the library in blocked form with one endmarked input head and all work heads within \\([-(\\lambda_m+1),\\lambda_m+1]\\), where \\(\\lambda_m=\\lceil\\log(m+2)\\rceil\\). The blocking width and finite library list are fixed before source-specific compilation.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>From a source code and positive supplied bounds \\(a,b\\), finite syntactic operations construct a total deterministic simulation of \\(A\\) on a virtual padded input. When the source satisfies those bounds, padding preserves its acceptance probability exactly, and the generated machine has explicitly computable simultaneous bit-space and bit-time bounds.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 11.1 gives one resource-capped randomized interpreter on every input, without a bounded-error promise for arbitrary encodings. Proposition 10 at fixed exponent \\(3\\), followed by comparison with \\(1/2\\), gives a total deterministic separator \\(A\\), since the approximation error is at most \\(1/8\\).</p><p>Fix this finite library once. Consolidate input heads by logarithmic coordinate storage and scans, then block work cells by one fixed width so each work head has the stated \\(\\lambda_m+1\\) address bound. Symbol widths are later charged in bits; this is not free compression. Selecting this valid fixed library list precedes every compiler input and does not require discovering a separator or a space coefficient from arbitrary source semantics.</p><p>Lemma 11.2 embeds each promised source without changing acceptance probability. Lemma 11.3 compiles its virtual input by finite templates and derives explicit simultaneous bounds from the generated syntax. Formation of code, \\(P\\), transition tables, and the integers \\(K,H,c\\) terminates. No source error or resource promise is tested.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.1", "orig": "Lemma 13.2, “A total resource-capped interpreter.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "One fixed randomized machine \\(U\\) has polynomial worst-case running time and \\(O(\\log(m+2))\\) counted work bits on every binary input of length \\(m\\).", "statement": "<p>One fixed randomized machine \\(U\\) has polynomial worst-case running time and \\(O(\\log(m+2))\\) counted work bits on every binary input of length \\(m\\).</p><p>Let\n\\[\n\\lambda_m=\\lceil\\log(m+2)\\rceil,\\quad\nR(m,d_{\\mathrm{code}})\n=\\left\\lfloor\\frac{\\lambda_m}{100(d_{\\mathrm{code}}+1)^3}\\right\\rfloor.\n\\]\nOn a prefix\n\\[\n1^{d_{\\mathrm{code}}}0z\\,1^n0E_{d_{\\mathrm{code}}}(x),\n\\]\nit requires \\(1\\le d_{\\mathrm{code}}\\le\\lambda_m\\) and \\(R\\ge1\\), then simulates at most \\(m\\) source transitions, allowing work coordinates only in \\([-R,R]\\). Malformed data, forbidden moves, or failure to halt by the transition cap reject. Otherwise it returns the source decision. The suffix after the prefix has no parsing significance.</p>", "proof": "<p>Count \\(m\\) before interpretation. Bound the unary code header by \\(\\lambda_m\\), check eligibility before copying code or allocating storage, and validate that code, data-length header, and data are present. Parsing arithmetic uses \\(O(\\lambda_m)\\) bits; loops and allocations are checked against actual listed code, not implicit ID universes.</p><p>At most \\(d_{\\mathrm{code}}\\) tape strips, each with \\(2R+1\\) cells of \\(d_{\\mathrm{code}}\\) bits, require\n\\[\nd_{\\mathrm{code}}^2(2R+1)=O(\\lambda_m)\n\\]\nbits, since eligibility bounds \\(d_{\\mathrm{code}}^3\\) and \\(d_{\\mathrm{code}}^2R\\) by \\(\\lambda_m\\). Program, state, symbol tuples, coin block, coordinates, step counter, and scratch fit simultaneously. The input coordinate has magnitude \\(O(m)\\), hence logarithmic length. Fetch source symbols by rescanning encoded data, supplying the declared markers or blanks.</p><p>All routines are bounded scans and binary arithmetic over polynomial ranges with fixed nesting depth. Each transition and failed check takes polynomial time in \\(m+2\\), and at most \\(m\\) transitions occur. Initial halting and the last permitted reached state are checked, including \\(m=0\\).</p><p>For a source with a one-way reusable random cell, keep a finite-control cached bit: obtain one fresh bit each transition, use it only if cache invalid, retain the used bit on a stay, and invalidate on advance. Unused fresh bits are discarded. This preserves distribution without extra counted space or source transitions.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Exact padded preservation, Section 13.3.", "statement": "<p>Exact padded preservation, Section 13.3.</p><p>For a fixed source code and supplied positive \\(a,b\\), define\n\\[\nP=1000(d_{\\mathrm{code}}+2)^4(a+b+2),\n\\qquad N_{\\mathrm{pad}}=2^{PB_n}.\n\\]\nLet \\(y_x\\) be the length-\\(N_{\\mathrm{pad}}\\) string with prefix\n\\[\n1^{d_{\\mathrm{code}}}0z\\,1^n0E_{d_{\\mathrm{code}}}(x)\n\\]\nand zero suffix.</p><p>If the source halts in \\((n+2)^a\\) steps and uses at most \\(bB_n\\) counted bits on every random tape, then\n\\[\n\\Pr[U(y_x)\\text{ accepts}]=\\Pr[\\mathcal M(x)\\text{ accepts}].\n\\]\nAll interpreter eligibility and resource checks pass.</p>", "proof": "<p>The prefix length is\n\\[\nL_{\\mathrm{pre}}=(d_{\\mathrm{code}}+1)(n+2)\\le N_{\\mathrm{pad}},\n\\]\nusing \\(2^{B_n}\\ge n+2\\) and \\(2^{P-1}\\ge d_{\\mathrm{code}}+1\\). Also\n\\[\n\\lambda_{N_{\\mathrm{pad}}}=PB_n+1.\n\\]\nThe padding choice gives\n\\[\nR(N_{\\mathrm{pad}},d_{\\mathrm{code}})\n\\ge30(a+b+2)B_n-1\\ge(a+b)B_n+4.\n\\]\nSince \\(P&gt;a\\),\n\\[\n(n+2)^a\\le2^{aB_n}&lt;N_{\\mathrm{pad}}.\n\\]\nLocal movement to a work cell visits every intervening counted cell, so the supplied source space bound places all work coordinates within the strip; the slack covers a final one-cell move. This uses original source cell costs, not larger interpreter IDs. No cap changes any promised run, proving exact equality.</p><p>The optional head conventions of original Remark 13.3 may be fixed in the coding and interpreter before selecting \\(A\\): up to \\(d_{\\mathrm{code}}\\) heads per tape require\n\\[\nO(d_{\\mathrm{code}}^2\\log(2R+1)+d_{\\mathrm{code}}^3)=O(\\lambda_m)\n\\]\nbits. Optional signed \\(R\\)-bit input or uncharged blank-tape coordinates also fit; padded source coordinates never overflow because their time-bound encodings fit \\(R\\ge aB_n+4\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Virtual-input compilation and explicit simultaneous bounds, Sections 13.4–13.5.", "statement": "<p>Virtual-input compilation and explicit simultaneous bounds, Sections 13.4–13.5.</p><p>Fix the finite library \\(A\\) of Proposition 11 and source-specific \\(P,z\\). A finite syntactic construction gives a total machine \\(D\\) simulating \\(A(y_x)\\) without writing \\(y_x\\).</p><p>From the generated finite-alphabet table compute:</p><p class=\"li\">\\(t\\): work-tape count;</p><p class=\"li\">\\(g\\ge2\\): maximum work-alphabet size;</p><p class=\"li\">\\(q_D\\): state count;</p><p class=\"li\">\\(u\\ge1\\): an integer dominating transition-table bit length, all alphabets, states, and tapes.</p><p>Set\n\\[\n\\rho=100(P+2),\\quad\nS=1+(t+1)(2\\rho+1)(g+2),\n\\]\n\\[\nK=4S,\\qquad c=2S+1,\\qquad\nH=2^{(u+100)^2}(q_D+1).\n\\]\nThe same execution obeys\n\\[\n\\operatorname{space}_{\\mathrm{bit}}(D,x)\\le K\\log(n+2),\n\\qquad\n\\operatorname{time}_{\\mathrm{bit}}(D,x)\\le H(n+2)^c.\n\\]\nThese totality and resource conclusions hold even if source promises fail.</p>", "proof": "<p>Inline \\(A\\)'s finite transition list with its own work tapes. During input callbacks retain its state in finite control. Four auxiliary low-end-first words suffice: \\(n+1\\), \\(N_{\\mathrm{pad}}+1\\), virtual head \\(J\\), and prefix counter \\(I\\).</p><p>Initialize \\(n+1\\) by incrementing once per real symbol. Its bit length is exactly\n\\[\n\\operatorname{bitlength}(n+1)=\\lceil\\log(n+2)\\rceil=B_n.\n\\]\nFor each bit place write \\(P\\) zeros and append one, giving \\(2^{PB_n}\\); increment for the virtual right marker. Fixed repetitions are hardwired control.</p><p>At a virtual read, first test \\(J=0\\) or \\(J=N_{\\mathrm{pad}}+1\\). Otherwise regenerate the prefix: emit fixed \\(1^{d_{\\mathrm{code}}}0z\\), scan \\(x\\) to emit \\(1^n0\\), then rescan to emit encoded data. “Emit” compares \\(I\\) with \\(J\\), returns the matching bit, or increments \\(I\\). A completed unmatched prefix returns zero. Rewind the real input by its endmarker, not a stored head coordinate. All words have at most \\(PB_n+2\\) bits and heads visit positions only through \\(PB_n+10\\). Counter carry, comparison, ended flags, and fixed symbol tuples are finite-control data. Library tapes remain live and untouched through callbacks; there is no callback stack.</p><p>Every work head lies in \\([-\\rho B_n,\\rho B_n]\\), both for blocked library tapes and auxiliaries. Encoding symbols in at most \\(g\\) bits bounds tape data by\n\\[\nt(2\\rho B_n+1)g\\le SB_n.\n\\]\nAllowing the stated factor two and \\(B_n\\le2\\log(n+2)\\) gives \\(K\\log(n+2)\\).</p><p>For fixed real input, complete configurations number at most\n\\[\nq_D(n+2)(2\\rho B_n+1)^t g^{t(2\\rho B_n+1)}\n\\le q_D(n+2)2^{SB_n}\n\\le q_D(n+2)^c.\n\\]\nAll virtual positions are already counted in the word \\(J\\). Callback loops terminate and \\(A\\) is total, so \\(D\\) halts and cannot repeat a complete configuration.</p><p>Implement finite-alphabet transitions by fixed-width binary blocks, blank encoded by zeros, with phases and scanned tuples in finite control. No new writable per-block delimiter is needed. A deliberately loose per-transition bound is \\(100(u+1)^8\\), dominated by \\(2^{(u+100)^2}\\). Multiplying by the macro-configuration bound gives the claimed bit time. This counts initialization, arithmetic scans, callbacks, and library operations simultaneously, including \\(n=0\\).</p>"}], "edges": [[0, 1, "cite"], [0, 2, "cite"], [5, 8, "cite"], [5, 23, "cite"], [5, 70, "cite"], [6, 5, "cite"], [7, 5, "cite"], [8, 15, "cite"], [8, 21, "cite"], [8, 27, "cite"], [8, 30, "cite"], [8, 43, "cite"], [8, 70, "cite"], [9, 8, "cite"], [9, 10, "cite"], [9, 12, "cite"], [10, 8, "cite"], [10, 11, "cite"], [10, 12, "cite"], [11, 8, "cite"], [11, 13, "cite"], [12, 8, "cite"], [13, 8, "cite"], [13, 14, "cite"], [14, 8, "cite"], [15, 27, "cite"], [15, 36, "cite"], [15, 43, "cite"], [15, 50, "cite"], [16, 15, "cite"], [17, 15, "cite"], [17, 31, "cite"], [18, 15, "cite"], [19, 15, "cite"], [19, 20, "cite"], [19, 21, "cite"], [20, 15, "cite"], [21, 15, "cite"], [22, 15, "cite"], [23, 27, "cite"], [23, 43, "cite"], [24, 23, "cite"], [24, 25, "cite"], [25, 23, "cite"], [25, 26, "cite"], [26, 23, "cite"], [27, 36, "cite"], [27, 43, "cite"], [27, 45, "cite"], [27, 49, "cite"], [28, 27, "cite"], [28, 30, "cite"], [29, 27, "cite"], [29, 30, "cite"], [30, 27, "cite"], [30, 33, "cite"], [30, 34, "cite"], [31, 27, "cite"], [31, 33, "cite"], [32, 27, "cite"], [33, 27, "cite"], [33, 35, "cite"], [34, 27, "cite"], [34, 35, "cite"], [35, 27, "cite"], [36, 43, "cite"], [36, 50, "cite"], [37, 39, "cite"], [38, 39, "cite"], [39, 41, "cite"], [40, 36, "part"], [41, 36, "cite"], [42, 36, "cite"], [43, 70, "cite"], [44, 46, "cite"], [45, 46, "cite"], [45, 50, "cite"], [46, 43, "part"], [47, 50, "cite"], [48, 49, "cite"], [48, 50, "cite"], [49, 50, "cite"], [50, 43, "cite"], [51, 68, "cite"], [51, 69, "cite"], [51, 70, "cite"], [52, 51, "cite"], [52, 54, "cite"], [52, 65, "cite"], [53, 51, "cite"], [53, 54, "cite"], [53, 55, "cite"], [53, 57, "cite"], [54, 51, "cite"], [54, 55, "cite"], [54, 56, "cite"], [55, 51, "cite"], [56, 51, "cite"], [57, 51, "cite"], [57, 58, "cite"], [58, 51, "cite"], [59, 51, "cite"], [60, 70, "cite"], [61, 67, "cite"], [62, 60, "cite"], [62, 63, "cite"], [62, 64, "cite"], [63, 60, "cite"], [64, 60, "cite"], [64, 68, "cite"], [65, 60, "cite"], [65, 68, "cite"], [66, 60, "cite"], [66, 67, "cite"], [67, 60, "cite"], [68, 60, "cite"], [68, 69, "cite"], [69, 60, "cite"], [70, 0, "cite"], [70, 1, "cite"], [70, 2, "cite"], [70, 3, "cite"], [70, 4, "cite"], [70, 72, "cite"], [71, 70, "cite"], [72, 4, "cite"], [73, 72, "cite"], [74, 4, "cite"], [74, 72, "cite"], [75, 4, "cite"], [75, 72, "cite"]]});
